Throughout the analysis, we considered solutions as channels if they satisfy the following conditions: (i) reached a parameter norm larger than $10^3$ (see \autoref{app:simulations2} for the definition of parameter norm) and, (ii) a minimum cosine distance between input weights $1-\cos(\inner{\ve{w}_i}{\ve{w}_j}/\|\ve{w}_i\|/\|\ve{w}_i\|)$ of less than $0.01$. See \autoref{app7} for a version of panel a in \autoref{fig5} computed for all datasets.
The fraction of update parallel to the \gammaline{} in \autoref{fig5}d was computed as $||\Delta\ve\theta_{\text{projected}}||/||\Delta\ve\theta||$ for each network that we deemed have reached a channel. 
We can see that our procedure to identify channels works well, as the majority of the late updates are parallel to the \gammaline{}. 
Yet, some networks do not show parallel updates to \gammalines{}, this is likely because they were mis-classified as channels due to the thresholding we used to identify channels, see \autoref{app8} for a quantification of this for all datasets.
The analysis of \autoref{fig5} was performed only by detecting closest pairs of input weights, but we noticed that some channel solutions contained triplets or even higher numbers of weights that were close to each other, leading to multi-dimensional channels. It also often happens that multiple pairs of weights are close to each other, leading to multiple one-dimensional channels.
The quantification of \autoref{fig5}d was computed only on the \gammaline{} generated by the closest pair, triplets or other channels were not considered. This results in some networks having a fraction of update parallel to the \gammaline{} between zero and one.
To detect multi-dimensional channels we fixed a set threshold of $0.01$ for the cosine distance between input weights, we then created groups of neuron indices in which each element satisfies the distance threshold with at least one element in the group. 
We ran this procedure on all networks that exceeded the maximum parameter norm. The size of these groups is reported in \autoref{fig5}g for all datasets. 
We used these groups to compute an improved version of \autoref{fig5}d, where we computed the fraction of updates parallel to the the multi-dimensional \gammaline{} (or lines if multiple groups were found) for each network.
The results of this analysis are shown in \autoref{app10}, we can see that by including multiple channels and multidimensional channels, the fraction of updates parallel to \gammalines{} increases significantly. 
Finally, to detect channels between layers of deep MLPs in \autoref{fig5}f, we used the same two criteria described at the beginning of this paragraph.


