Local environments within a protein are highly diverse, due to the variability of the sequence of amino acids and the fold of the amino-acid chain to a 3D object. Computationally representing these inherently heterogeneous, dynamic, and chemically diverse environments is challenging, yet very important: many key properties of proteins critically rely on these local enviroments, for example the ability of a protein to bind a ligand or perform enzymatic activity. Dynamics of a protein is ultimately rooted in the packing in 3D space, and is generally considered crucial for function. In this context, \textit{biomolecular nuclear magnetic resonance} (NMR) spectroscopy is a powerful technique, allowing to probe protein dynamics and interactions at atomic resolution.

The principal NMR observables are \textit{chemical shifts} of certain atomic nuclei endowed with non-zero spin (e.g.,$^1$H, $^{13}$C, $^{15}$N atoms). The chemical shift of an atom is exquisitely sensitive to its local electronic environment and is critically dependent on local structural features, such as the type of amino acid, the dihedral angles between bonds (and, thus, secondary structure), the orientation of side chains, hydrogen bonding, and solvent exposure. These parameters, averaged over time, impact the chemical shift in a complex manner, and while the chemical shift is exquisitely sensitive to them, interpreting chemical shifts in terms of structure is hampered by this complexity.

There is a strong need for accurate computational prediction of chemical shifts, with enormous potential in several directions: accurately predicted chemical shifts from a given protein structure largely facilitates the assignment of observed NMR signals to individual atoms; moreover, the inverse direction of determining structures and dynamics from experimentally observed chemical shifts hinges on the ability to predict chemical shifts with high accuracy.
%facilitates spectrum assignment––the primary bottleneck in biomolecular NMR workflows, and requires precise atomic-level representations of the protein environment. %It however requires precise atomic-level representations of the local protein environment.

Existing chemical-shift prediction approaches~\cite{neal2003shiftx, shen2008sparta,ucbshift2023} use experimentally determined chemical shifts and experimental 3D structures; these structures are parametrized with hand-crafted descriptors to represent the local environment (e.g. dihedral angles, hydrogen bonds, ring-current shifts). Likewise, predictors of the protonation state of titratable groups equally employ hand-crafted features~\cite{olsson2011propka3}. These tools face limitations in adaptability and accuracy. This highlights the need for more expressive, effective, and generalizable representations of local protein environments.
%While backbone-based representations (e.g., backbone coordinates, dihedral angles) are commonly used to model local structure, they fail to encode the full atomic context surrounding residues. On the other hand, while side-chain angles provide richer structural information, they do not explicitly capture inter-residue interactions. Classical methods for chemical shift prediction~\cite{neal2003shiftx, shen2008sparta}, and protonation state prediction~\cite{olsson2011propka3} use hand-crafted descriptors to represent the environment and face limitations in adaptability and accuracy. This highlights the need for more expressive, effective, and generalizable representations of local protein environments.

In computational chemistry, descriptors like Parrinello-Behler symmetry functions \cite{behler2011atom, behler2007generalized, behler2016perspective} and Smooth Overlap of Atomic Positions (SOAP) are widely used to represent molecular environments \cite{jager2018machine}. These methods encode atomic interactions and geometry into concise, invariant representations suited for geometric modeling. Modern atomistic machine learning techniques \cite{schutt2018schnet, schutt2021equivariant} implicitly learn similar representations within neural network layers, achieving accuracy comparable to density functional theory (DFT) \cite{deng2023chgnet} in tasks like molecular dynamics (MD) simulations.

