\section{Background on Atomistic Foundation Models }\label{sec:sec_background}

% \subsection{LocoHD}
% The LocoHD \label{background:locohd} \cite{fazekas2024locohd} metric measures local similarity between two molecular or atomic structures by comparing their surrounding atomic environments. It represents each atom’s neighborhood as a histogram counting the types of atoms within expanding shells around the atom, capturing the local chemical composition at multiple scales. The metric computes distances between pairs of anchor atoms by integrating and comparing these histograms over the expanding shells.

Force field models approximate a system’s potential energy from atomic positions and types. The gradient of this energy with respect to atomic positions defines the forces acting on each atom. Traditional force fields were modelled by empirically computing interatomic potentials or using DFTs to analytically determine interatomic forces \cite{finnis2003interatomic}. More recently, AFMs, have been developed to approximate the energy function and force fields from computational quantum mechanical data. In this work, we use the following AFMs (detailed summary in Table~\ref{tab:descriptor_info} in Appendix):

\noindent \textbf{MACE} \cite{batatia2022mace, kovacs2023mace}\label{background:mace} is based on the body-ordered Atomic Cluster Expansion (ACE) \cite{dusson2022atomic} with higher-order (many-body) equivariant message passing to capture complex atomic interactions. It constructs atomic energies using symmetry-preserving basis functions and updates them through rotation-equivariant neural interactions. MACE is trained on datasets containing small molecules and tripeptides to predict the energies and forces of DFT relaxation trajectories. Introduced recently, \textbf{Egret} \cite{wagen2025egret} \label{background:Egret} shares a similar higher-order equivariant message-passing architecture with MACE, but introduces architectural optimizations that improve computational efficiency and scalability.
\smallskip

\textbf{OrbNet} \cite{qiao2020orbnet} \label{background:orb}  enhances graph neural networks by incorporating quantum-derived features calculated using fast approximate quantum chemistry methods. These features include symmetry-adapted atomic orbital overlaps and elements of the Fock matrix \cite{szabo1996modern}, which capture important information about the electronic structure. OrbNet is trained on DFT relaxation trajectories and quantum chemistry datasets (containing small molecules and materials) to predict molecular energies and properties.
\smallskip

\textbf{AIMNet} \cite{zubatyuk2019accurate} \label{background:aiment} uses message passing over atomic environment descriptors and iterative updates inspired by charge equilibration schemes. Through these updates, AIMNet captures both short- and long-range interactions and builds a joint, information-rich representation of each atom in its molecular context. The model is trained on diverse quantum mechanical datasets to predict molecular energies, forces, and other properties across diverse chemical systems.


\section{Representing protein local environments with AFMs}\label{sec:sec_env}
\noindent \textbf{Notation.} Denote a residue in a protein as ${a}$, and the all-atom representation of the protein structure as $\mathcal{X} = \{ (\mathbf{x}_{1}, z_1) \dots (\mathbf{x}_{m}, z_m) \}$, where $\mathbf{x}_{j} \in \mathbb{R}^3$ and $z_j$ are the location and atomic number of $j^{th}$ atom in $\mathcal{X}$, respectively. Let $f_\theta: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{Y}$ be the AFM with parameters $\theta$, and $\mathcal{Y} = \{ (\mathbf{y}_1, z_1), \ldots, (\mathbf{y}_m, z_m) \}$ the atom-wise feature representations of the AFM, where $\mathbf{y} \in \mathbb{R}^d$. 
The \textit{local environment} of a \textit{focus residue} ${a}$ is denoted by $\mathcal{X}_{{a}} \subseteq \mathcal{X}$. The subset of atoms in residue ${a}$ used for predicting biochemical properties is denoted as $\mathcal{A}_{a}$. Given a subset of atom indices $\mathcal{A}_{a}$, we denote by $\mathcal{Y}_{\mathcal{A}_a} = \{\, (\mathbf{y}_i, z_i) \mid i \in \mathcal{A}_a \} \subseteq \mathcal{Y}$ the restriction of $\mathcal{Y}$ to those atoms.

%Finally, the dataset of protein structures is defined as $\mathcal{D}$.

%AFMs model the potential energy of a system of $m$ atoms as a function of both bonded and non-bonded atomic interactions.  we already have that in the background
We seek a representation of a local protein environment to be (i) sensitive to local changes, (ii) insensitive to global variations, (iii) fast to compute, (iv) canonical, enabling direct comparison across diverse environments, and (v) effective and generalizable to unseen environments. 
Encoding full proteins with several thousand atoms with AFMs is computationally inefficient and redundant since we are solely interested in locally sensitive features. 
To construct such representations, we propose to construct a local environment around a {focus residue} to be encoded later by the AFM.



\textbf{Creating local environments.} To balance computational efficiency while retaining local structural context, given a focus residue $a$, we construct the environment $\mathcal{X}_{a}$ as the union of all residues whose atoms are at most $5${\AA} away from the atoms of ${a}$. The procedure is described in Algorithm ~\ref{alg:env_extract}.

%AFMs are trained on small molecules or short peptides and capture the potential energy of the entire structure. Extending this idea to larger proteins remains a challenge as this approach is computationally inefficient and redundant.   Hence, we propose to focus on local chemical environments around specific amino acids to capture chemical context in proteins more efficiently. In the following, we describe the construction of these \textit{local environments}, which serve as input to the network in place of the complete structure $\mathbf{X}$.

\textbf{AFM representations.} 
Given an environment $\mathcal{X}_a$, AFMs produce atom-level features over the layers of the network. These learned atomistic features are \textit{contextual}, influenced not only by the atom itself but also by the other atoms in the environment due to the message-passing operations within AFMs.  %Contextualized representations of a given atom $\mathbf{x}_{i}$ within an amino acid $\mathbf{a}$ depend on capturing relevant information from its local surroundings
Given $\mathcal{X}_a$, atom-wise feature representations are extracted from the final layer of the AFM. 
Each AFM produces representations of shape $N \times d$, where $N = |\mathcal{X}_{a}|$ and $d$ is the dimension of the representation. In order to construct representations that are \textit{canonical}, i.e., comparable across residues, the representations corresponding to atoms in $\mathcal{A}$ are retained, yielding contextualized representations of their local chemical environments per residue. Given a protein structure, this process is performed for each residue in the sequence, and residue-wise features are curated. A visual depiction of this procedure is presented in Fig.~\ref{fig:flow}.

\section{AFM representations capture local protein structure and chemistry}
\label{sec:chemistry}
% Learning meaningful representations of atomic environments is key to modeling protein structure and function. By accurately modeling the interatomic energy landscape, AFMs can encode the local geometric arrangements, such as amino acid configurations and secondary structure motifs. Beyond geometric configurations, these models capture physicochemical information relevant to properties like acid dissociation constants (pK\textsubscript{a}). 
In what follows, we investigate how AFM representations of local environments can be leveraged to predict a range of structural and chemical properties.

\textbf{Amino acid identity \& secondary structure.}  A protein's \textit{primary structure} is a linear sequence of amino acids connected by covalent peptide bonds. The twenty standard proteinogenic amino acids \cite{kawashima2000aaindex} share a backbone of four heavy atoms: the $\alpha$-carbon (CA), amide nitrogen (N), carbonyl carbon (C), and carbonyl oxygen (O), with variable side chains (R-group). A protein's \textit{secondary structure} is a local motif of backbone conformation, stabilized by hydrogen bonds between atoms of non-adjacent amino acids \cite{kabsch1983dictionary}, with the most common types being $\alpha$-helices (H) and $\beta$-strands (E).


To examine the information encoded by the AFM representations, the atomistic features from MACE were projected into a two dimensional space using Uniform Manifold Approximation and Projection (UMAP) \cite{mcinnes2018umap}. The resulting embeddings, shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:umaps}, are annotated with amino acid labels, secondary structure, and dihedral angles. Notably, features corresponding to $\alpha$-helices and $\beta$-strands form distinct clusters in the UMAP space. A similar pattern of separation is observed when embeddings are annotated by amino acid identity and backbone dihedral angles, indicating that chemically and structurally distinct features are well-represented in the feature space.


%\textbf{Quantitative Evaluation.} 
To quantitatively assess this capability, we train classifiers to predict the secondary structures and the amino acid identities. Further details on the loss function, target label space, and the definition of $\mathcal{A}_a$ can be found in Appendix~\ref{subsec:amino_ss_appendix}, while a comprehensive description of the model architecture is provided in Appendix~\ref{appendix:app_model_arch}. Classifier accuracy was evaluated using precision, recall, and F1 scores. Results for secondary structure prediction are summarized in Table~\ref{tab:ss_results}, while amino acid classification results are presented in Table~\ref{tab:name_results}. For secondary structure, models trained with MACE and Egret features consistently achieve superior prediction on average. For amino acid prediction, models trained using Egret features consistently outperform those trained with alternative representations.

\textbf{Acid dissociation constants (pK\textsubscript{a}).}
pK\textsubscript{a} quantifies the tendency of a titratable group (such as the carboxylic acid group of an Asp side chain) to donate a proton, serving as a measure of its acidity. When the solvent pH is lower than pK\textsubscript{a}, the molecule will be primarily in its protonated form, while being increasingly deprotonated at pH higher than pK\textsubscript{a}. The pK\textsubscript{a} value is the pH value at which the group is half protonated over the ensemble of molecules. Although each amino acid has a canonical pK\textsubscript{a} value in isolation, its actual value within a protein can deviate due to variations in the local chemical environment. External factors such as (i) the presence of nearby charged residues, polar atoms, or functional groups, and (ii) limited solvent accessibility—particularly in buried regions—can influence protonation behavior. This makes pK\textsubscript{a} prediction an ideal test case for evaluating whether atomistic features can encode meaningful local chemical information.

%\textbf{Quantitative Evaluation.} 
For quantitative assessment, a regression model was trained on amino acids with ionizable, proton-transferring side chains to predict their pK\textsubscript{a} values. Ground-truth pK\textsubscript{a} values were estimated using a Poisson–Boltzmann solver \cite{reis2020pypka}. The evaluation focused on glutamic acid (GLU) and aspartic acid (ASP), which tend to deprotonate, and lysine (LYS) and histidine (HIS), which tend to protonate. Additional training details are provided in Appendix~\ref{appendix:sec_4}. As shown in Table~\ref{tab:pka_mae}, models trained with AIMNet features outperform those using alternative features because it is trained to predict multiple molecular properties simultaneously. All AFM-based regressors outperform conventional baselines such as PropKA \cite{olsson2011propka3} and pKa-ANI \cite{gokcan2022prediction}.


% \textbf{Note:} In addition to pK\textsubscript{a}, we trained regressors to predict several other atomic-level chemical properties. However, these tasks are comparatively simpler than predicting an amino acid’s pK\textsubscript{a}, which is more sensitive to subtle variations in the local chemical environment. A summary of the results for these additional properties is provided in Table \ref{tab:chem_prop_mae}.

