\section{Interpreting AFM representations}
\label{sec:interpretation}
While atomistic features from AFMs excel at representing local protein structure and chemistry, it remains unclear how their internal representations respond to structural perturbations or whether they encode meaningful physical properties. To address this, the behavior of AFM representations under specific conformational changes is examined. Such changes induce smooth, interpretable trajectories in the embedding space, suggesting that the features capture physical motions and respect constraints such as locality, continuity, and symmetry. These properties are explored in the following case studies. 

\textbf{Rotated phenylalanine sidechain aromatic ring.} 
%A synthetic trajectory of a fixed protein environment is constructed by rotating a single phenylalanine side-chain from $-180^{\circ}$ to $180^{\circ}$ (Fig.~\ref{fig:ring_pca}). 
Using the synthetically modified phenylalanine described above, we perform PCA on the MACE embeddings obtained at every $\chi_2$ angle for the nearby CA atoms (residues $50$ and $51$ of PDB ID: $\texttt{1ZV6}$). We observe that the first two principal components trace a smooth, one-dimensional curve, capturing the expected $180^\circ$ periodicity of the rotation. However, the effect diminishes with distance: residue $49$ begins to deviate from the periodic pattern, while distant residue $40$ exhibits no discernible structure, illustrating the spatial locality of the descriptor’s sensitivity.

\textbf{Helix unfolding.} In context of MD simulations of helix-to-strand transition described earlier (Fig.~\ref{fig:helix2sheet_pca}), we perform PCA on the MACE embeddings of CA atoms in residues $6$ and $7$, and obseve that they follow smooth, non-intersecting trajectories. These trajectories reflect the gradual unfolding process, indicating that the descriptor space encodes continuous and interpretable structural transitions.

Further exploration of reconstructing protein environments from AFM embeddings (inverse embedding) is presented in Appendix.~\ref{appendix:guidance}.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

Recent work has highlighted the versatility of pretrained AFMs as learned representations for molecular tasks. \cite{shiota2024universal} use MACE and M3GNet features to predict chemical shifts in small molecules, while \cite{elijovsius2024zero} employ MACE embeddings for zero-shot molecular generation via evolutionary search. Unlike these small-molecule applications, our method is specifically designed for proteins — large, structurally diverse biomolecules requiring environment-specific representations to capture their complex local chemistry. Similar to our idea, \cite{gokcan2022prediction} predict protein pK\textsubscript{a} values using ANI-2x AFM representations; as shown in Table~\ref{tab:pka_mae}, our approach outperforms theirs in pK\textsubscript{a} prediction accuracy.


\section{Discussion}
\label{sec:discussion}
In this work, we introduced a new representation for local environments in proteins, and demonstrated its effectiveness across applications. While our downstream models used frozen AFM embeddings, task-specific fine-tuning of AFMs is a promising direction that may yield further gains. Moreover, our chemical shift prediction for HA, while accurate, still slightly lags behind UCBShift, pointing to opportunities for future improvement. Notwithstanding these limitations, we believe that the proposed fully differentiable chemical shift predictor can be used to guide AlphaFold and similar generative models \cite{maddipatla2025inverseproblemsexperimentguidedalphafold} for determining structure from experimentally measured chemical shifts -- an important task in protein NMR that has so far been hindered by the complexity of structure-to-chemical-shift relation.

% However, it has two main limitations: (1) it relies on first training an AFM model and then using its features, rather than an end-to-end approach; and (2) while chemical shift prediction for hydrogens is effective, further improvements are needed, likely requiring additional exploration and refinement of the environment construction.


\textbf{Societal impact.} The work presented here attempts to advance protein representation, since proteins are fundamental ingredients of life and are implicated in health and disease, the potential impact on basic and applied research may be profound. We do not see any special ethical concerns worth highlighting.

