\begin{example}\label{L}
   The corresponding Laplacian matrix $L$  of a directed  graph Figure \ref{graph22} is 
$$L=\left(\begin{array}{ccccc}4 & -1 & -1 & -1 & -1 \\ -1 & 3 & -1 & 0 & -1 \\ -1 & 0 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\ -1 & 0 & -1 & 3 & -1 \\ -1 & -1 & 0 & 0 & 2\end{array}\right).$$
It can be seen that $L=W-\one v^T$, where  $$W = \left(\begin{array}{ccccc}5 & -1 & -1 & -1 & -1 \\ 0 & 3 & -1 & 0 & -1 \\ 0 & 0 & 2 & -1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0 & -1 & 3 & -1 \\ 0 & -1 & 0 & 0 & 2\end{array}\right)$$
 and  the vector $v = [1,0,0,0,0]^T$. 
\end{example}

According to Section \ref{lap}, we know that $L^{\frac12}=W^{\frac{1}{2}}- \one y^T$, where $y=(W^{-\frac12})^Tv$. We compute $W^{\frac12}$ and  $W^{-\frac12}$ by DB iteration and obtain 
$$y=[0.4772 \quad 0.1097\quad  0.1667\quad   0.1097\quad   0.1667]^T.$$


On the other hand, if we suppose $L=W^{\frac12}-\one x^T$, then $x$ solves equation 
\begin{equation}\label{lap3}
    (I+V^T)x-\|x\|_1x=v.
\end{equation}
Considering the function $g(\mu)=\|((1-\mu)I+V^T)^{-1}v\|_1-\mu $, where $\mu\in [0,1]$, then according to Section \ref{lap}, we know that the function $g(1)=0$ and it can be verified that $1$ is a  double root. This way, the Newton iteration and SDA may not be applied directly. Indeed, both Newton iteration and SDA have linear convergence, and the computed $x$ is far away from the exact solution $y$. We made some changes to Newton iteration and obtain 
\begin{equation}\label{improvednewton}
\mu_{k+1}=\mu_k-2\frac{g(\mu_k)}{g'(\mu_k)}, \qquad \mu_0=0,
\end{equation}
where $g'(\mu_k)=\|((1-\mu_k)I+V^T)^{-2}v\|_1-1$. 

