\caption{Improved Newton iteration \eqref{improvednewton} for computing the solution of equation \eqref{lap3}}
\label{fig:Laplace_Newton}
\end{figure}
{Figure \ref{fig:Laplace_Newton}} shows the convergence behaviour of the improved Newton iteration \eqref{improvednewton}. We obtain ${\|x_N-y\|_1=8.7931e-12}$, where $x_N$ is the  solution computed by Newton iteration and $y$ is the exact solution obtained by $y=(W^{-\frac12})^Tv$. 




\begin{remark}
    As we can see from Example \ref{L}, for a general vector equation $Ax-\|x\|_1x=b$, Newton iteration and SDA may not apply. We leave this general problem as a future investigation. 
\end{remark}

\section{Conclusions}

We investigated the theoretical and computational properties of a vector equation $Ax-\|x\|_1x=b$, where $A=(2-\|a\|_{\w})I-T_n(a)$ is an invertible $M$-matrix of Toeplitz structure, and $b$ is a nonnegative vector.  We showed that such equation has a unique solution $x_*$ such that $\|x_*\|_1+\|a\|_{\w}<1$ under the condition that $\|b\|_1<(1-\|a\|_{\w})^2$. Such solution is of interest when related to the square root of certain  invertible $M$-matrix with a rank-1 correction.  We proposed a relaxed fixed-point iteration and Newton iteration for the computation of such solution. Moreover, we showed that a structure-preserving doubling algorithm can be applied in computing the solution, the convergence rate is at least 1/2. 






