As a second sample, a pillar from the enamel of a human primary tooth is imaged. The enamel consists mainly of hydroxyapatite crystals \cite{Robinson2004}. They have a width of \SI{30}{\nano\meter} to \SI{70}{\nano\meter} and a length of \SI{100}{\nano\meter} to \SI{1000}{\nano\meter} \cite{Wang2006}. The crystals bundle into rods, which are about \SI{6}{\micro\meter} in diameter \cite{Fosse1968}. The crystals and the edges of the rods create a structured dark-field signal. Figure \ref{fig:tooth_direc_df} shows the directional dark-field projection of the tooth sample. The outer edges of the rods dominate the dark-field image, and their orientation can be well separated, as seen in the zoom-in region. Besides the edges, there is also a dark-field signal coming from within the rods. This signal originates from the crystalline structure of the hydroxyapatite. Note how the orientation, and therefore the color, changes from the bottom left to the top right. The change in color could be related to the change in the orientation of the crystals within a rod. Figure \ref{fig:tooth_direc_df} shows the rods as circular structures with an opening on one side, known as a key-hole structure. This opening is pointing roughly at \SI{45}{\degree} towards the bottom left in the lower left part of the tooth. However, moving to the top right part of the tooth, these openings gradually point more towards the bottom. This shift in the orientation of the prism also affects the orientation of the crystals within. When looking at the color bar, a change from the red (bottom left) to green (top right) corresponds roughly to a change of the observed \SI{45}{\degree}. This is a strong indication that the directional dark-field signal coming from within the prism is sensitive to the change of orientation of the hydroxyapatite crystals. Also, note that the solid support structures on the top and right side are completely dark and do, therefore, not contain any scattering structures.

