The CA transition rules tested in this work are described in Table~\ref{tab:rules}. A majority rule---the first one listed in the table---is very simple: if a given cell has $N$ or more live neighbors in a Moore neighborhood of radius $r$, then it lives on to the next generation; otherwise, it does not \cite{toffoli1987cellular}. In turn, the Caves rule is well known within games PCG games research, having been proposed by Johnson et al. for the purpose of generating 2D cave levels in real time \cite{johnson2010cellular}. It is a slightly tweaked majority rule, in which a cell is able to survive (i.e., considering it is already alive) by having one less live neighbor than what it requires for being born (i.e., if it is dead). The Diamoeba rule has a gap in the neighbor interval required for cell birth, forming large diamonds with unpredictable boundaries when observed in its 2D domain \cite{gravner1998cellular}. Finally, the Coral rule further limits when a cell is born, leading to slow growth and producing a coral-like texture when viewed in 2D. 
These rules assume a Moore neighborhood, with parameterizable radius $r$ in the case of the Majority and Caves rules, and radius set to one ($r=1$) for the Diamoeba and Coral rules. 

