\section{Results, Discussion, and Limitations}
\label{sec:results}

Fig.~\ref{fig:experiments} shows several terrains generated with Algorithm~\ref{alg:hmgen} using various CA rules after the specified amounts of iterations. As can be observed, Majority rules are able to produce contrasting types of terrain depending on their parameterization. For $r=1$, terrain starts off highly irregular ($i=3$), smoothing out after several iterations, and stabilizing at $i=20$. While at $i=5$ and $i=10$, results are somewhat similar to  bumpy terrain with boulders, for $i=20$ and above, the generated landscape becomes essentially a flat surface with some soft irregularities.

Increasing the Majority rule radius to 2, as shown in the second row of Fig.~\ref{fig:experiments}, generates arguably more interesting results. For $i=5$, the generated terrain is akin to an eroded sedimentary landscape, with abrupt cliffs and several mesas. In contrast, $i=20$ yields a more diverse landscape, mostly composed of hills, but also having a few sharp cliffs. The softening effect with increasing iterations is still present, offering a consistent way of defining terrain abruptness. 

The Majority rule still holds some surprises for $r=4$. Setting the survival/birth threshold to $N=38$ yields an landscape with irregular holes, potentially useful as a surface for worn-out objects. Setting $N=43$ produces the opposite effect, yielding irregular hills. Comparing it to the previous case of $r=2$, it is possible to conclude that in this case the main terrain features standout sooner (i.e., for lower $i$), while maintaining their prominence and various minor irregularities for longer (i.e., for higher $i$).

Results for the Caves rule are particularly interesting, as they closely follow their 2D counterpart. The rule was tested with $r=2$ and neighbor threshold $N=13$, with results shown in the fifth row of Fig.~\ref{fig:experiments}. From a stacked 3D perspective, the 2D caves become eroded landscapes, increasingly well-defined during the iterative process. Contrary to the similarly parameterized Majority rule ($r=2$, $N=13$), the eroded landscape is cleaner and holds its shape for longer.

The chaotic Diamoeba rule is also able to produce natural-looking landscapes. While terrains seem somewhat rugged for $i<50$, the rule comes on its own for $i \geq 100$, yielding diverse surfaces with hills, canyons, mesas, as well as smaller yet smooth features. Finally, the Coral rule appears to map its 2D output---coral-like features---onto the 3D domain. This is particularly clear for $i=200$.

