Fig.~\ref{fig:other} shows additional results one can obtain with the technique. Fig.~\ref{fig:others:majr2n13} displays a Majority rule similar to what is shown in the second row, rightmost column of Fig.~\ref{fig:experiments} ($r=2$, $N=13$, $i=50$). The difference is in the seed used for generating the initial noise and the larger $h_{\max}$, resulting in a generally soft but distinct alien-looking landscape with prominent features. Fig.~\ref{fig:others:majr4n41} highlights a Majority rule with $r=4$ with $N=41$, a compromise between the perforated terrain of $N=38$ and irregular hills obtained with $N=43$, producing a mix of mostly flat terrain with extrusive and well-defined features. The landscape shown in Fig.~\ref{fig:others:cavecombo} depicts a combination of three separate terrains added together. These were generated with different parameterizations of the Caves rule. The first two layers, defining the finer details of the landscape, used $r=1$, $N=5$, and $seed=123$; the first one is obtained from $i=3$ with a very small $h_{\max}$, while the second was collected with $i=50$ and normalized to a larger height. The third layer, generated with $r=2$, $N=5$, a different seed, and higher $h_{\max}$ than the previous two, sets up the coarser aspects of the landscape. Finally, Fig.~\ref{fig:others:dia500} and Fig.~\ref{fig:others:dia050} offer two additional perspectives on the Diamoeba rule: the former is a stepper, further iterated version of the natural-looking Diamoeba landscape already presented in Fig.~\ref{fig:experiments} (row before last, rightmost image), while the latter also increases height but uses a different seed to produce a rocky-like terrain with various features.

We believe the preliminary results presented here are interesting in themselves, some with aesthetic pleasing features, others doing an arguably good job of mimicking real world landscapes or surfaces. However, these results barely scratch the surface of what is possible with the proposed technique. Among innumerable CA transition rules, only a few were experimented with here, and all of them seeded with an initial grid of random noise. Tweaking the initial probability of live cells will surely yield distinct results, as well as using predefined initial shapes, which some rules respond better to in the 2D case \cite{toffoli1987cellular}. As shown in the example of Fig.~\ref{fig:others:cavecombo}, combining together different heightmap generators---not only CA generators, but also common ones such as Perlin noise---holds the potential for further customization of the produced landscapes. Finally, 2D CAs with small neighborhoods offer good performance and can be GPU-parallelized \cite{cagigas2022efficient}, making them suitable for real-time map and level generation \cite{johnson2010cellular}.

This work presents some limitations nonetheless. Besides only having experimented with some rules---it is an exploratory early work---no objective analysis or validation (in-game or otherwise) has been performed. Analysis is limited to subjective aesthetic considerations by the authors. Further, the constructive nature of CAs may pose a challenge in understanding how parameters influence the final output, complicating control over specific gameplay features such as playability or map connectivity. Consequently, achieving desired game characteristics may require additional preprocessing or iterative testing \cite{yannakakis2018artificial}.

\section{Conclusions and Future Work}
\label{sec:conclusions}

In this paper we presented a method for generating terrains by adding sequential iterations of 2D CAs to a heightmap and then normalizing it. Results are diverse and interesting from an aesthetic point of view, although no objective analysis has been performed given the preliminary nature of this work. Besides performing such a validation in the future, we aim to experiment with different rules, contrasting initial states (e.g., rectangles or other shapes), and compare generation times against those of other methods. Although still a work in progress, we believe the proposed method has potential for real-time generation of terrains, textures, and surfaces.

