##########################CONCLUSOES###########################
\par In Refs.\cite{ref1}\cite{ref2} the authors have suggested the existence of a novel type of magnetic levitation, that uses two permanent  neodymium magnets of similar size.  The system is composed of one  magnet,  rotor(R),  mounted on a motor with its north and south poles oriented perpendicular to the rotation axis.  Where  the second magnet, floater(f), is placed in the vicinity of the rotating magnet. 

\par The argument for characterization of a novel type of magnetic levitation arises from observation that the spinning rate of the Levitron is $\sim O(500) rpm$\cite{ref7}, much lower than seen in this device as suggested in\cite{ref1}.  However, the magnets used in the device described in the paragraphs above correspond to neodymium magnets and as we have seen, they lead to exactly another scale for the angular velocity of the spinning top.

\par The experimental results obtained for $\omega_f$, described in the
 Table I,  are in agreement with  the estimate  based on rescaling arguments 
 given by Eq.(\ref{eqesc}) and  corroborate with to  suggestion  that the novel type of magnetic levitation presented  in\cite{ref1}\cite{ref2} , can actually be seen as a scaled-up version of a Levitron, which we call "Dynamical  Levitron".

\par Considering the discussion presented throughout subsections (A) and (B), we saw that in analogy with the behavior of a usual Levitron, where the observed levitation effect is only achieved when the ratio of the mass to the magnetic moment $m_/\mu$ is correct, through necessary adjustments  as  adding small weights to  the spinning top. In  the dynamical  Levitron case, the corresponding correction that must be incorporated into the system formed by the rotor and the float, is mediated by the parameter $\delta_R$ who will be responsible for the necessary adjustment of the float  precession axis to magnetic field direction, as well as the necessary condition for   the magnetic interaction to counterbalance the weight force under the float. 




