the observations listed in the paragraph above, suggests  a $\delta^{max}_R$ value. Thus, the maximum value reached by $R_f = R^{max}_f$ should be defined by $\delta^{max}_R$.

par As we have seen, Eq.(\ref{eq11a}) is capable of satisfying the condition (i) assuming  that $3z_f/(z_R - z_f) < 1$. However, 

\par The limit at which  $z_f \to 0$, is also capable of meeting condition (i), these case  can be seen as the limit at which the float is precisely described by a point dipole. On the other hand,

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
\hspace*{-0.5cm}\includegraphics[scale=0.35]{plot/figures/fig2-d07a.png}\hspace*{1cm}\includegraphics[scale=0.35]{plot/figures/fig2-d07b.png}
    \caption{Contour plot obtained for Eqs.(\ref{eq8.1}) and  (\ref{eq11d}), for $\delta_R \approx 0.7mm$ for a cylindrical float with diameter and height of $5mm$,  mass $m_f = 0.73gr$ with 
    magnetic momentum of float and rotor $\mu_f = \mu_R = 0.087 A.m^2$ . The contextualization of the curves behavior are described in the text.}
    \label{fig2}
\end{figure}

%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\par In order to complement the analysis of the case addressed in this section, in Table II we show the results obtained for a spherical float, with diameter $D=7mm$ and mass $m_f =1.53gr$. In comparison with the data presented in Table I, it is possible to identify an increase in $R_f(sph)$, where $R_f(sph) \propto O(2) R_f(cyl)$. 

\par This difference in behavior can be addressed by observing the following fact, the mass of the sphere is almost $2\times$ greater than the mass of the cylindrical float, with the effect of compensating for this increase in weight, and also indirectly the change in the moment of inertia, the increase in $R_f(sph)$ is observed.

 \par During the confinement of the float, the system is in dynamical equilibrium and presents  vertical stability, being characterized by the interaction energy given  by Eq.(\ref{eq8.1}). In order to maintain dynamical stability,
the dipolar interaction term, which is proportional to  $\propto (3\delta_R/d - R_f)$ , must then compensate for the  $O(2)$ factor mentioned above. Therefore, the increase observed in $R_f(sph)$, has as its counterpart the decrease in $d$ of order  $O(2)$,  to satisfy the equilibrium condition of the system.
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%


\subsection{Lateral displacement - $\delta_R \approx 1mm$}

\par In this subsection we will consider the case in which the lateral displacement of the center of the magnet in relation to the rotor axis, illustrated in FIG.(2b), corresponds to $\delta_R \approx 1mm$. The choice to treat this situation as a separate case from the one previously discussed arises from a subtlety necessary for the correct characterization $\delta^c_R$, obtained with the help of Eq.(\ref{eqs}), for the results presented in Table III.

\par  As we have seen, the parameter $\delta_R$ is responsible for controlling the levitation dynamics of the float. Depending on the shape of the float (which will effectively have different masses and radius $\mu_f/m$ ), we can observe different behaviors for $R_f$ and $d$, as illustrated in FIGs. (4) and (5). If we consider, for example, the comparison between the results for a float with the same geometry (spherical in this case), however, trapped in traps generated by different configurations of $\delta^{{A,B}}_R  (0.7mm, 1mm)$, 
we can also expect different behaviors for $(R_f(A), R_f(B))$ and $(d(A) , d(B))$.

\par The difference  that we mentioned above arises from the fact that different choices of $\delta_R$, lead to different energy scales for trapping the float  which we can denote by  ($E_A$) and ($E_B$) , in order to treat the rotor configurations in which $\delta_R=0.7mm$ and $\delta_R=1mm$, in terms of the different interaction energies involved between the float and rotor.

\par  Assuming the limit $z_f \to 0$, again noting that the dipolar term   is  $\propto (3\delta_R/d - R_f)$, with a similar argument to that presented at the end of the previous section, we can expect that the radius between the interaction energy of these two systems behaves like

\begin{equation}
    \frac{E_A}{E_B} \approx \frac{\delta_ R(E_A)}{\delta_ R(E_B)}. 
    \label{eqres}
\end{equation}

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
\hspace*{-0.5cm}\includegraphics[scale=0.35]{plot/figures/fig3-d07a.png}\hspace*{1cm}\includegraphics[scale=0.35]{plot/figures/fig3-d07b.png}
    \caption{Contour plot obtained for Eqs.(\ref{eq8.1}) and  (\ref{eq11d}), for $\delta_R \approx 0.7mm$ for a spherical float with diameter $7mm$,  mass $m_f = 1.53gr$ with 
    magnetic momentum of float $\mu_f = 0.18  A.m^2$ and 
    rotor $\mu_R = 0.087 A.m^2$ . The contextualization of the curves behavior are described in the text.}
    \label{fig5}
\end{figure}

\par Thus, now the prediction for $\delta^c_R(E_B)$, defined on the energy scale $E_B$ where $\delta_R \approx 1mm$,   can be designed appropriately for the energy scale $E_A$ defined by the configuration where $ \delta_ R \approx 0.7mm$,  by the following expression

\begin{equation}
   \delta^c_R(E_A) \approx \delta^c_R(E_B)\frac{\delta_ R(E_A)}{\delta_ R(E_B)} = \delta^c_R(E_B)\frac{E_A}{E_B} . 
    \label{eqres2}
\end{equation}

\par Considering Eq.(\ref{eqs}), the estimate obtained for $\delta^c_R(E_B)$ assuming the data reported in Table III, results in  $\delta^c_R(E_B)(sph)  \approx O(1)mm$. The prediction obtained for $\delta^c_R(E_B)(sph)$, according to the above discussion, is justified due to systems $\delta_R   \approx 0.7mm$  and $\delta_R \approx 1mm$ presenting different energy scales $E_A$  and $E_B$ associated with the trapping of the spherical float, thus, considering Eq.(\ref{eqres2}), the correct estimate for $\delta^c_R(E_A)(sph)$ evaluated on the energy scale $E_A$, assuming the data reported in Table III, would be $\delta^c_R(E_A)(sph) \approx O(0.7)mm$, which, even though approximate, indicates agreement with the results obtained with the data of Table II.

\par FIG.(6) shows how systems ($\delta_R = 0.7mm$) and ($\delta_R = 1mm$), for the same interval $z_f \supset (0,16)mm$, present different energy scales for trapping,  $E_A$ and $E_B$. Taking the  energy values  defined by the  blue region, we can improve the previous estimate to $\delta^c_R(E_A)(sph) \approx O(0.53)mm$ and then estimate the average value  obtained with the data from the mentioned tables as $\bar{\delta}^c_R(E_A)(sph) = 0.48mm$. 

\par Based on the discussion we have presented in this section, we can define $\delta^c_R(E)$ as the minimum value, which is established at the lowest energy configuration $E$, that allows a given float to satisfy simultaneously the trapping conditions (i) and (ii). 

\par Finally, we can now consider the possibility of the existence of a maximum value for $\delta_R$, that is, $\delta^{max}_R$. The discussion that we presented in the previous paragraphs shows that an increase in $\delta_R \to \delta_R(E) + \Delta \delta_R$ is responsible for the increase in $R_f$, considering of course that the same float is subject to the action of this shift.

\par The condition imposed for potential energy to be a minimum at the trapping point,  $U_{z}(z_f) > 0$, establishes the following condition for $\delta_R$ 


\begin{eqnarray}
&& \delta_R  \lesssim \frac{(dR_f)_{max}}{6}\frac{(2d - 3h)}{(d - 2h)}, 
\label{eqmax1}
\end{eqnarray}

\noindent in Eq.(\ref{eqmax1}), the value assigned to $(dR_f)_{max}$  in this case,  corresponds to the situation that the float must now present the maximum possible value for rotation speed, capable of satisfying the stability conditions. As a consequence, the distance between the rotor and the float $d$ is the minimum possible , i. e. $d\to h(D)$, which leads to  
\begin{eqnarray}
&& \delta_R  \lesssim \frac{(R_f)_{max}}{6}h(D). 
\label{eqmax1}
\end{eqnarray}

\begin{figure}[t]
    \centering
\hspace*{-0.5cm}\includegraphics[scale=0.35]{plot/figures/fig4-E07.png}\hspace*{1cm}\includegraphics[scale=0.35]{plot/figures/fig4-E1.png}
    \caption{Contour plot obtained for Eq.(\ref{eq8.1}), for a spherical float with diameter $7mm$,  mass $m_f = 1.53gr$ with  $\mu_f = 0.18  A.m^2$ and  $\mu_R = 0.087 A.m^2$ for cases (a) $\delta_R \approx 0.7mm$  and  (b) $\delta_R \approx 1mm$.}
    \label{fig6}
\end{figure}

\par And we can estimate $\delta^{max}_R$ by assuming that  $R^{max}_f\to 1$, which corresponds to the limit at which $\omega_f \to \omega_R$, and sets the following bounds for the floats that we consider in this work $\delta^{max}_R(cyl)  \lesssim 0.83mm$ and  $\delta^{max}_R(sphl)  \lesssim 1.2mm$.

\subsection{The ratio  between the  minimum  and maximum  frequency values}


\par  As we mentioned, for the dynamical  Levitron  the transfer of angular momentum from the base, which rotates, to the float is mediated by the configuration of magnetic field lines provided by the positioning of the magnet coupled to the rotor. As a result, the system formed by the float and rotor now becomes coupled, with the magnitude of interaction between them being determined by the parameter $\delta_R$, responsible for defining the energy scale $E$, where we observe the trapping of the float $\delta^{c}_R(E)$


\par In this way, from the determination of $\delta^{c}_R(E)$ and $\delta^{max}_R(E)$, we can then estimate the ratio  between the  minimum(min)  and maximum(max) values  allowed for  $\omega_f(E)$ and $\omega_R(E)$, for the floats considered in this work. The direct use of  Eq.(\ref{eqs}) and  Eq.(\ref{eqres2}) , implies the following radius obtained for $\omega^{min}(E)$


\begin{eqnarray}
&& \frac{\omega^{min}_f(E)}{\omega^{min}_R(E)} \approx  \frac{5\delta^c_R (E)}{d} .     
\label{eqmax3}
\end{eqnarray}
\noindent  That in the case of the cylindrical float, where $E\to E_A$ corresponds to

\begin{eqnarray}
&& \frac{\omega^{min}_f(E_A)}{\omega^{min}_R(E_A)}(cyl) \approx  \frac{5\delta^c_R (E_A)}{d} \approx   \frac{1.5mm}{d},    
\label{eqmax3b}
\end{eqnarray}

\noindent being the corresponding result for the spherical float evaluated at $E\to E_A$ and $E\to E_B$ , given  by

\begin{eqnarray}
&& \frac{\omega^{min}_f(E_A)}{\omega^{min}_R(E_A)}(sph) \approx  \frac{5\delta^c_R (E_A)}{d} \approx   \frac{2.4mm}{d} \\ 
&& \frac{\omega^{min}_f(E_B)}{\omega^{min}_R(E_B)}(sph) \approx  \frac{5\delta^c_R (E_B)}{d} \approx   \frac{5mm}{d}.
\label{eqmax3c}
\end{eqnarray}

\par 

\par  As we commented in the introductory section, in Ref. \cite{ref3} the authors showed that 
 the ratio between the possible frequencies to the spinning top are limited to the interval defined by 

\begin{equation}
\frac{\omega_{min}}{\omega_{max}} \approx \frac{1}{3}. 
 \label{eqRatio}   
\end{equation}

\noindent The comparison between the predictions of Eqs.(\ref{eqRatio}) and (\ref{eqmax3}), shows  that the  system we have named "dynamical  Levitron", indeed behaves  similarly to a Levitron, being the ratio  between the maximum and minimum values  allowed for $R_f$ very similar. 



\par As a result, the system formed by the float and rotor now becomes coupled, with the magnitude of interaction between them being determined by the parameter $\delta_R$. Therefore, the prediction obtained by (\ref{eqmax3}) results for the  dynamical  Levitron in the following the ratio between the  minimum frequencies of the float $\omega^{min}_f$ and rotor $\omega^{min}_R$, permitted by conditions (i) and (ii)

\begin{eqnarray}
&& \omega^{min}_{f}(cyl)  \approx 0.28\frac{h}{d} \omega^{min}_{R}(cyl) \nonumber    \\
&& \omega^{min}_{f}(sph)  \approx 0.36\frac{D}{d} \omega^{min}_{R} (sph), 
\label{eqfreqmin}
\end{eqnarray}
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
\par By performing the experiment correctly, it is possible to identify that due to oscillations in the rotational speed $\omega_R$ of the rotor, which we can denote by $\delta \omega_R$, that these oscillations effectively lead to disturbances in $R_f$, which will potentially be responsible for the instability of the trap, as reported in Ref.\cite{ref1}. 

#####################################################

\par  The  Eq.(\ref{eqRat234}) illustrates how the adjustment required for the observation of magnetic trapping in the dynamical Levitron is controlled by the lateral displacement $\delta_R$.


###################################################


\par  With the results reported in Tables (II-VII)   we can estimate the region in the plane $(z_f(mm),R_f)$ where the conditions (i)  and (ii), are  contemplated for the floats employed in the experiments. In FIG.(6) we present  a contour plot  of $U_z =U_{f}(z_f)$ and $\partial^2_{z}U_z$  for corresponding case, where in this plot we too consider the same choices adopted for the graph shown in FIG.(3). 


\par From the graphs depicted in FIG(6), assuming $d = 10 mm$  which is equivalent to considering $z_f = 6mm$, it is possible to estimate that the measures described in Table I correspond to the region located by the green symbols, where the sphere data are represented by $({\bf\color{green}{\centerdot}}, {\bf\color{green}\bullet})$ , while  the data for cylindrical float by $({\bf\color{green}{\blacksquare}})$.


\par By performing the experiment correctly, it is possible to identify that due to oscillations in the rotational speed $\omega_R$ of the rotor, which we can denote by $\delta \omega_R$, that these oscillations effectively lead to disturbances in $R_f$, which will potentially be responsible for the instability of the trap.  Finally,  as illustrated in figures (6a) and (6b)  the conditions (i)  and (ii), are simultaneously satisfied.

where we now have a new type of  spinning top, "the dynamical spinning top" which can be identified as the floater(f),  being in this type of system the base and the spinning top coupled , which suggests the term "dynamical Levitron" , allowing  new possibilities for the phenomenon of magnetic levitation.

############################################################################

\par At the end of the previous subsection we mentioned the experimental evidence of the existence of an upper limit $\delta^{max}_R \approx 2.5mm$ , where the magnetic trapping effect was not observed for higher values tested for lateral displacement. 

\par  In order to establish the existence of a possible upper bound for $\delta_R$, we can reconsider Eq.(\ref{eq8.1}) and verify   for which choices of lateral displacement we obtain $\vec{\,\,\,\,\,\nabla U}_z = 0$. 

\par In FIG.(4), considering a series of measurements that were carried out in which $R_f = 0$,  we identify that the critical value for the lateral displacement $\delta^c_R(0)$  is defined for the region in which $z_f \approx 4mm$ or $d\sim O(10)mm$. 

\par With this result in hand, where $\delta^c_R(0)$ is characterized by $d \sim O(10)mm$,  in FIG.(6) the red dashed-dotted describe 
$\vec{\,\,\,\,\,\nabla U}_z = 0$ obtained  for spherical floater assuming the data reported in Table I for $R_f\sim 0.125$. At point where $d\approx O(9.7)mm$ ,  this curve intercept the solid black line that corresponds to $\delta^c_R \sim 0.24mm$ at a maximum point, therefore,  we can expect that the maximum value admitted for  $\delta_R = \delta_R^{max}$ , can then be obtained by extrapolation in the limit in which $R_f \to 1$ at $d\approx O(10)mm$.

\par  So, in this same figure the orange dashed-dotted line indicate  that at $d\approx O(10)mm$  the dynamical stability condition can only be satisfied if  $ \delta^{max} _R\lesssim 2,4mm$ which is in accordance with the experimental evidence. However, we should point out that the maximum point of the function occurs at $d\approx 14mm$ as illustrated by the orange dashed-dotted  curve , although, $d\approx 14mm$ indicates that  $z_f  \to 0$ and  $\delta^{max} _R$ is then determined in the limit  of a point dipole.

\par At this point, we can reconsider the discussion conducted below from Eq.(\ref{eqs}), where we saw that  corrections due to the finite size of the float tend to decrease the prediction of the dipolar approximation by a factor $ \lesssim O(10\%$) which leads to $\delta^{max} _R \approx O(2,6)mm$, which allows us to infer from the discussion presented in the last two paragraphs that the upper limit established for lateral displacement must be included in the interval
$2,4mm\lesssim \delta^{max} _R \lesssim  O(2,6)mm$.



###########################################################################
\par As the data reported in Tables (II-VII) illustrate, for the situations in $R_f > 0$, $d \lesssim O(d_{max})$ is observed, so that we can consider Eq.(\ref{eqRat}) as  reasonable to  describing the observed trapping in the reported situations.
