\section{Introduction}

Steinberg algebras were introduced in  \cite{Steinberg2010} as algebras with coefficients in the field 
$\C$ of complex numbers. They are constructed from certain topological groupoids and have proven useful in purely algebraic settings as abstract models for inverse semigroup algebras \cite{Steinberg2010}, Leavitt path algebras \cite{LPAbook}, and Kumjian–Pask algebras \cite{CP}. Complex Steinberg algebras have also found applications in functional analysis, for example in \cite{BCFS} or more recently in \cite{BGHL}; they sit densely inside the associated groupoid C*-algebra \cite{snake}. The ability to move between algebraic and analytic frameworks has become a valuable technique for making progress in both areas.

At first,  the specific choice of $\C$ as field of coefficients did not appear to be particularly significant in the algebraic setting. However, when the groupoid is not Hausdorff, the study of the simplicity of such algebras \cite{Simplicity, Nek, StSz, StSz2} has made it clear that the choice of the field of coefficients, and more precisely its characteristic, plays a crucial role.

Given a field $K$, recall that a Steinberg algebra over $K$ is defined as the $K$-linear span of characteristic functions of compact open sets.  In a non-Hausdorff groupoid, we may have compact sets that are not closed, and so the associated Steinberg algebra may contain functions whose support has empty interior: such functions are called \textit{singular functions}. From \cite[Theorem~3.14]{Simplicity}, we know that a Steinberg algebra that contains singular functions cannot be simple. 

In this paper, we begin to study the relationship between the characteristic of the field $K$ and the existence of proper non-zero ideals inside the ideal of singular functions of the Steinberg algebra. We consider the Steinberg algebras of the non-Hausdorff groupoids known as the {\it $n$-headed snake}, for the cases where $n$ is either $2$ or $3$. %In the future we aim to study Steinberg algebras defined over fields of arbitrary characteristic, with a special focus on their ideals of singular functions.

The two-headed snake $\GGG_{2S}$ \cite[Example~2.1]{snake} is one of the most basic examples of a non-Hausdorff groupoid. For us, the body of the snake comprises the well-known Hausdorff Cantor set, which is the unit space of this groupoid. The fact that the groupoid $\GGG_{2S}$ is non-Hausdorff is concentrated at one location: the two heads of the snake, denoted as $0$ and $1$,  which interact with each other as in $\mathbb{Z} / 2 \mathbb{Z} $. The open sets of $\GGG_{2S}$ are the open sets of the Cantor set together with copies of those sets with head $0$ replaced by head $1$. 
%{\color{cyan}{In a sense, the two-headed snake is more like a snake (a Cantor set) with two interchangeable heads. Any non-Hausdorff behaviour comes from sets that contain both heads.}} 
In this paper, we show that the singular functions of the associated Steinberg algebra $\AAA_{K}(\GGG_{2S})$ are precisely those with support equal to $\{0,1\}$ -- that is, functions that are non-zero precisely on the heads of the snake. 

In a Steinberg algebra $\AAA_{K}$, the set $\SSS_{K}$ of singular functions is an ideal. We are interested in whether this ideal is {\it $S$-simple} -- that is, whether $\SSS_{K}$ has non-trivial proper subsets that are also ideals of $\AAA_{K}$.  We show that the ideal $\SSS_{K}(\GGG_{2S})$ of singular functions in the Steinberg algebra $\AAA_{K}(\GGG_{2S})$ is $S$-simple, regardless of the field $K$. 

Next, we add a third head, denoted $2$, such that the three heads interact with each other as $\mathbb{Z}/3 \mathbb{Z}$: this is the three-headed snake groupoid $\GGG_{3S}$. Again, the fact that $\GGG_{3S}$ is non-Hausdorff is confined to the heads, and we prove that the singular functions of the associated Steinberg algebra $\AAA_{K}(\GGG_{3S})$ are those whose support is contained in $\{0,1,2\}$. However, with three heads, the $S$-simplicity of $\mathcal{S}_K(\mathcal{G}_{3S})$ now depends on the choice of field $K$. Indeed, we prove that $\mathcal{S}_{K}(\mathcal{G}_{3S})$ is not $S$-simple if, and only if, $K$ is a \textit{splitting field} for $x^2+x+1$ -- that is, iff $K$ contains roots of $x^2+x+1$. 

In particular, this means that whenever the field $K$ is algebraically closed (e.g. when $K = \C$, or $K$ is an algebraic closure of a finite field), the ideal $\mathcal{S}_{K}(\mathcal{G}_{3S})$ is never $S$-simple. 
Also, if $p = 3$ or if $p$ is a prime congruent to $1$ modulo $3$, then $\Zmodp$ is a splitting field for $x^2+x+1$, and thus so too is any field of characteristic $p$. %for which $\SSS_{K}(\GGG_{3S})$ is also non-$S$-simple. 
%Also, if $K$ is a subfield of $\mathbb{C}$, then $K$ is a splitting field for $x^2+x+1$ if and only if $\mathbb{Q} (\sqrt{3}i) \subseteq K$. RATHER AS A REMARK IN THE BODY OF THE PAPER ?

We also prove that when $\mathcal{S}_{K}(\mathcal{G}_{3S})$ is not $S$-simple, it contains precisely two proper subset ideals (resp. one proper subset ideal), associated with the roots of $x^2+x+1$, when the characteristic of $K$ is different from $3$ (resp. is equal to $3$). 

This paper focuses on the two- and three-headed snakes because the group structures of the heads -- $\mathbb{Z} / 2 \mathbb{Z} $ and $\mathbb{Z} / 3 \mathbb{Z} $ respectively -- are the most straightforward nontrivial options and provide already interesting properties. In future work, we will study arbitrary $\Zmodn$-headed snakes, and plan to reach further to any (abelian, finite-type) group-headed snake. 
%Another example of interest is the `cross' groupoid of \cite[Example~3.5]{CEP} from \cite[Section~2]{NonHGpds}. In a sense, it is the `dual' of the two-headed snake, as it has two Hausdorff `bodies' which are connected in the middle by a single non-Hausdorff `head'. 

\section{Preliminaries} 

\subsection{Ample groupoids and Steinberg algebras}  

This paper involves a detailed study of two particular groupoids: the two-headed snake $\GGG_{2S}$ and the three-headed snake $\GGG_{3S}$, along with the Steinberg algebras associated to each. 
 We will work entirely in these two special cases, but provide some brief preliminaries for context.  For a more detailed presentation of groupoids and Steinberg algebras, the interested reader can for example see \cite{Steinberg2010}. %and could almost proceed without presenting the more general definitions.  However, considering that this paper is the first of a series that will explore more general instances of non-Hausdorff groupoids,

A \textit{groupoid} $\mathcal{G}$ is a generalisation of a group where the binary operation is only partially defined. As a result, the identity, or `unit', is not a single element, but a set of elements.  We write $\mathcal{G}^{(0)}$ for the set of all units in $\mathcal{G}$.  Each $\gamma \in \mathcal{G}$ has an inverse $\gamma^{-1} \in \mathcal{G}$ and we write $\textbf{r},\textbf{s}:\mathcal{G} \to \mathcal{G}^{(0)}$ for the maps
\[\textbf{r}(\gamma)=\gamma\gamma^{-1} \quad \text{ and } \quad \textbf{s}(\gamma)=\gamma^{-1}\gamma \ , \]
which we call the \emph{range} and \emph{source} maps respectively.  Composition between two elements $\gamma$ and $\eta$ in $\mathcal{G}$ is defined if, and only if, $\textbf{s}(\gamma) = \textbf{r}(\eta)$. 

The motivating example for this paper is a bundle of groups.  That is, let $X$ be a nonempty set and, for each $x \in X$, let $\Gamma_x$ be a group.  Then: 
\[\mathcal{G} = \bigsqcup_{x\in X}\Gamma_x\] is a groupoid: a pair of elements is composable if, and only if, there exists an $x\in X$ such that both elements are in $\Gamma_x$; then composition is defined as composition in the group $\Gamma_x$. 

 A {\it topological groupoid} is a groupoid equipped with a topology such that composition and inversion define continuous maps. 

 Steinberg algebras are constructed from certain topological groupoids that generalise the class of discrete groups.  First, we say an open set $B$ in a topological groupoid $\mathcal{G}$ is an \emph{open bisection} if the source and range maps restrict to homeomorphisms from $B$ onto an open subset of $\mathcal{G}^{(0)}$.
 Then, a topological groupoid is \emph{ample} if its topology is generated by a basis of compact open bisections. We always require the unit space of our groupoids to be Hausdorff, although the main point of our investigation here is that the groupoid itself need not be Hausdorff.  

Now, let $\mathcal{G}$ be an ample groupoid with Hausdorff unit space. The \textit{Steinberg algebra} of $\mathcal{G}$ over a field $K$ is the $K$-algebra $\AAA_K(\mathcal{G})$ of functions $f : \mathcal{G} \rightarrow K$ such that:  
    \[ f = \sum\limits_{B \in F} a_B 1_B \ , \] 
    where $F$ is a finite collection of compact open bisections $B$ of $\mathcal{G}$ and, for all $B \in F$, $a_{B} \in K$ and $1_{B}$ denotes the characteristic function of $B$. %that is:
%   \[
%  1_B(\gamma) = \begin{cases}
%      1&\text{ if }\gamma \in B;\\
%        0&\text{otherwise.}
%    \end{cases}
%   \] ISN'T IT A STANDARD DEFINITION ? (TO ME IT IS, THUS THE QUESTION)

    Addition and scalar multiplication in $\AAA_K(\mathcal{G})$ are defined pointwise, and   multiplication in the algebra is given by the following convolution formula:
    \[ \forall \  f, g \in \AAA_K(G), \ \ \ \ \ (f*g)(\gamma) := \sum\limits_{\mathbf{r}(\eta)= \mathbf{r} (\gamma)} f(\eta) g(\eta ^{-1} \gamma) \ . \] 


Note that, for compact open sets $U$ and $V$, convolution of their characteristic functions reduces to $1_{U}*1_{V}=1_{UV}.$
Further, if we assume $U,V \subseteq \mathcal{G}^{0}$, then the range and source maps are the identity maps; hence, $U$ and $V$ are necessarily open bisections.  In this case, convolution of their characteristic functions becomes:
\[ 1_{U} * 1_{V} = 1_{U \cap V} \ . \] 
% When it is clear which groupoid we are working with, we will simply write $A_K$ for the Steinberg algebra.  


\subsection{Ideals of Singular Functions} 
When $\mathcal{G}$ is Hausdorff, any function that belongs to $\AAA_K(\mathcal{G})$ is continuous.  However, as we will see in the next section, if $\mathcal{G}$ is not Hausdorff, $\AAA_K(\mathcal{G})$ can contain some functions that are not continuous.\\
For a function $f:\mathcal{G} \to K$, we define the \emph{support} of $f$ by: 
\[
\supp(f)\coloneqq \{\gamma \in \mathcal{G} : f(\gamma) \neq 0\}.
\]
This is sometimes referred to as the `open support' when the function $f$ is continuous. However, if $f$ is not continuous, then $\supp(f)$ is not necessarily open.  We say a function $f \in \AAA_K(\mathcal{G})$ is \emph{singular} if $\supp(f)$ has empty interior.  We denote by $\mathcal{S}_{K}(\mathcal{G})$ the set of all singular functions in $\AAA_K(\mathcal{G})$:
    \begin{equation*}
    \mathcal{S}_{K}(\mathcal{G}) := \{ f \in \AAA_K(\mathcal{G}) : \supp(f) \text{ has empty interior} \}.
    \end{equation*} 
%When it is clear which groupoid we are working with, we will simply write $\mathcal{S}_K$. 
We know from \cite[Proposition~3.7]{Simplicity} that $\mathcal{S}_{K}(\mathcal{G})$ is an ideal\footnote{Meaning a two-sided ideal: $\forall f \in \mathcal{S}_{K}(\GGG), \ \forall g \in \AAA_K(\GGG)$, both $fg$ and $gf$ lie in $\SSS_{K}(\GGG)$.} in $\AAA_K(\mathcal{G})$.
A {\it singular ideal of $\AAA_K(\GGG)$} is a proper subset of $\SSS_{K}(\GGG)$ that is also an ideal of $\AAA_K(\GGG)$.

\begin{remark}
    If $I$ is an ideal of a ring $R$ and if $J$ is a subideal of $I$, then it is not true in general that $J$ is an ideal of $R$. This is why we are interested in proper subsets $J \subsetneq \mathcal{S}_{K}(\mathcal{G})$ that are themselves also ideals of the full algebra $\AAA_K(\mathcal{G})$, rather than in (subideals of) $\mathcal{S}_K(\mathcal{G})$. To reflect this, we call $\mathcal{S}_K(\mathcal{G})$ {\it not $S$-simple} when singular ideals $J$ exist, rather than `non-simple', which would imply subideals of $\mathcal{S}_K(\mathcal{G})$ itself. 
\end{remark}

\section{Singular ideals in the Steinberg Algebra of the Two-Headed Snake} 
\subsection{The Two-Headed Snake} 
The two-headed snake is a well-known example of a non-Hausdorff space. We study a totally disconnected version that has a groupoid structure, as in \cite[Example~2.1]{snake}.
Let $X$ be the Cantor set:  this serves as the body of our snake. Letting $l$ and $u$ denote two distinct digits\footnote{Most sources use $0$ and $1$ instead of $l$ and $u$, but that notation is prone to confusion here, so we avoid it.}, we can view each element of $X$ as an infinite sequence in $\{l,u\}^{\mathbb{N}} $. The topology on $X$ has a basis $\mathcal{B}_{X}$ consisting of cylinder sets $Z(\mu)$, for each finite sequence $\mu$ with entries in $\{l,u\}$, defined as: 
\begin{equation*}
    Z(\mu) := \left\{ \mu x : x \in \{l,u\}^{\mathbb{N}} \right\}. 
\end{equation*} 
In other words, the cylinder set $Z(\mu)$ consists of all infinite sequences  with initial subsequence $\mu$. Each cylinder set $Z(\mu)$ is compact in $X$, and $X$ is Hausdorff and compact for this topology.  

We write  $0$ for the element $lll... \in X$: this $0$ is the first `head' of our snake. We add another head, denoted by $1$. Note that this $1$ is not the point $uuu... \cong 1_{\mathbb{R}} \in X$, which sits at the other end of the Cantor set. 

