Thus, as a set, the two-headed snake groupoid is
$\mathcal{G}_{2 S} = X \cup \{ 1 \}.$
The composable pairs in $\GGG_{2S}$ are given by:
  \[
        \mathcal{G}_{2 S}^{(2)} = \Delta X \cup  \{ (0,1) , (1,0), (1,1) \} \ , \]
where $ \Delta X := \{ (\gamma,\gamma) : \gamma \in X \}$. 
The composition law is denoted by $+$ and defined by: 
\begin{align*} 
\gamma +\gamma &= \gamma \text{ for all } \gamma \in X,\\  
0+1&=1 \ ,\\ 
1+0&=1 \ , \\
1+1&=0 \ . 
\end{align*} 

Basic open sets of the topology on $\mathcal{G}_{2 S}$ are the basic open sets of the Cantor set $X$, plus a copy of each basic open set $B \in \mathcal{B}_{X}$ that contains the point $0$, with $0$ replaced by the other head $1$. That is:
    \begin{equation*}
        \mathcal{B}_{2 S} = \mathcal{B}_X \cup \{ (B \backslash \{ 0 \} ) \cup \{ 1 \} : B \in \mathcal{B}_X \text{ such that } 0 \in B \}.
    \end{equation*}
This groupoid can be viewed as a group bundle, consisting of trivial groups at every point in the body $X$ and one copy of $\mathbb{Z} / 2 \mathbb{Z} $ at the heads.  
As above, we call all of the elements in $\mathcal{B}_{2 S}$ {\it cylinder sets}, and we will use subscripts to denote whether a set contains the head 0 or the head 1, where relevant. In particular, $X_{0} = X$ is the snake with head 0 (and not 1) and $X_{1}$ is the snake with head 1 (and not 0). 

With this structure, $\mathcal{G}_{2 S}$ is an ample groupoid with Hausdorff unit space. The entire groupoid is not Hausdorff because the elements 0 and 1 cannot be separated by open sets.  Each element of the basis $\mathcal{B}_{2 S}$ is a compact open bisection, while any set that contains more than one head of the snake cannot be a bisection (as $\textbf{r}(1) = \textbf{r}(0) = 0$, the range map is not injective on such sets; likewise neither is the source map). Note that sets of the form $X_{a} \backslash Z(\mu)_{a}$, where $a=0$ or $1$, are also compact open bisections. 

\begin{lemma} \label{2h_formoffunc}
Let $K$ be a field and let $f \in \AAA_K(\mathcal{G}_{2 S})$. Then $f$ may be written in the form: 
\begin{equation*}  
    f = a_{0} 1_{X_{0}} + a_{1} 1_{X_{1}} + \sum\limits_{B \in F_{w}} a_{B} 1_{B} \ , 
\end{equation*} 
where $F_{w}$ is a finite collection of compact open bisections of $\mathcal{G}_{2S}$ that contain neither head of the snake, and $a_{0},a_{1},a_{B} \in K$ (possibly zero). 
\end{lemma} 

\begin{proof}
Begin by writing $f$ in the standard form: $f = \sum\limits_{B \in F} a_{B} 1_{B}$, where $F$ is a finite collection of compact open bisections of $\GGG_{2S}$. Without loss of generality, we can assume each $B \in F$ is a cylinder set (by decomposing $B$ into a disjoint union of basic open sets and writing $a_{B}1_{B}$ as the corresponding sum). 

Now, split $F$ into three disjoint parts: the collection of those $B$ that contain the head $0$ (denoted $F_0$); those that contain the head $1$ (denoted $F_1$); and those that contain neither head (denoted $F_w$). Then:
\[ f = \sum\limits_{B \in F_{0}} a_{B}1_{B} + \sum\limits_{B \in F_{1}} a_{B} 1_{B} + \sum\limits_{B \in F_{w}} a_{B} 1_{B} \ . \] 

We first consider $F_{0}$. Let $a_{0} := f(0) \in K$ (possibly zero) and for each $B \in F_{0}$, write: 
\[ a_{B} 1_{B} = a_{B} 1_{X_{0}} - a_{B}  1_{X_{0} \backslash B} \ ,\] 
where $X_{0} \backslash B$ is a compact open bisection, comprising the elements of $X_{0}$ that are not in $B$. Then we have: 
\[
    \sum\limits_{B \in F_{0}} a_{B} 1_{B} = \left(\sum\limits_{B \in F_{0}} a_{B}\right) 1_{X_{0}} + \left(\sum\limits_{B \in F_{0}} - a_{B}  1_{X_{0} \backslash B}\right).
\]
As the head $0$ is in each $B \in F_{0}$, we always have that $0 \notin X_0 \backslash B$, thus we can move the terms of $\sum\limits_{B \in F_{0}} - a_{B}  1_{X_{0} \backslash B}$ into the sum over $F_{w}$, with $F_{w}$ adjusted accordingly. Doing so, we obtain that the only remaining bisection in $F = F_{0} \cup F_{1} \cup F_{w}$ containing the head $0$ is $X_0$, so its coefficient $\sum\limits_{B \in F_{0}} a_{B}$ must be equal to $f(0) = a_{0}$. 

We can repeat the same process for the collection $F_{1}$, with $a_{1} = \sum\limits_{B \in F_{1}} a_{B}$, and moving the terms $\sum\limits_{B \in F_{1}} - a_{B}  1_{X_{1} \backslash B}$ into the sum over $F_{w}$ with $F_{w}$ adjusted accordingly. Hence, we have $f = a_{0} 1_{X_{0}} + a_{1} 1_{X_{1}} + \sum\limits_{B \in F_{w}} a_{B} 1_{B}$ as claimed. 
\end{proof} 


\subsection{The ideal of singular functions in $\AAA_K(\mathcal{G}_{2 S})$ } 

The main result of this section is \cref{prop:2hideals} below, which states that over any field, $\AAA_K(\mathcal{G}_{2 S})$ has no singular ideals. That is, $\SSS_K(\GGG_{2S})$ is always $S$-simple. 


First, observe that for a function $f \in \AAA_{K}(\GGG_{2S})$ to be singular, it must have no support on the body of the snake:  
\begin{lemma} \label{2h_singnosuppbody}
    A function $f$ is singular if and only if $supp(f) \subseteq \{0,1\}$. 
\end{lemma} 

\begin{proof}
    Let $f \in A_K(\mathcal{G}_{2S})$. Assume there exists $\gamma \in X \backslash \{0\}$ in $supp(f)$. 
Write $f$ in the form of \cref{2h_formoffunc}. 
Then there exists some compact open bisection $U \in F_w$ such that $\gamma \in U$. 
Let $F_{\gamma}$ be the subcollection of $B \in F_w$ that contain $\gamma$. Then: 
\[ V := U \cap \left( \bigcap\limits_{B \in F_{\gamma}} B \; \; \backslash \bigcap\limits_{B \in F_w \backslash F_{\gamma}} B \right) \] 
is an open set, and $V \subseteq supp(f)$. Thus $supp(f)$ does not have empty interior, so $f \notin \SSS_{K}(\GGG_{2S})$.  

Finally, note that $\{0,1\}$ contains no open sets, so if $supp(f) \subseteq \{0,1\}$ then $f$ is by definition singular. 
\end{proof}

Note that $\SSS_{K}(\GGG_{2S})$ is non-trivial: the function $h$ below is non-zero and singular: 
\[
h:=1_{X_{0}}-1_{X_{1}} \quad \text{satisfies} \quad
h(\gamma) = \begin{cases} 1&\text{if }\gamma= 0\\
-1 &\text{if }\gamma= 1\\
0&\text{otherwise}\end{cases} \ , 
\]


In fact, for $f$ to be singular we must have $supp(f) = \{0,1\}$:  
\begin{lemma} \label{singfuncform} 
    Let $f \in \mathcal{S}_K(\mathcal{G}_{2S})$. 
    Then $f$ can be written in the form $f = a (1_{X_{0}} - 1_{X_{1}})$, for $a = f(0) \in K$. 
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
    Let $f \in \SSS_{K}(\GGG_{2S})$ be a non-zero singular function (otherwise, $a = 0$ is suitable). With the notation of \cref{2h_formoffunc}, we have:
\[ \displaystyle        f = a_{0 }1_{X_{0}} + a_{1}  1_{X_{1}} + \sum\limits_{B \in F_{w}} a_{B} 1_{B} \ .
\]
    As $\supp(f) \subseteq \{0,1\}$, we can assume $F_{w} = \emptyset$, hence $f = a_{0} 1_{X_{0}} + a_{1}  1_{X_{1}}$. As $f$ is non-zero, at least one of the heads is in $\supp(f)$. Assume that $0 \in \supp(f)$ (the argument for $1$ is similar): then $a_{0} = f(0) \neq 0$. Now, choose $\gamma \in X_0 \backslash \{0\}$. Then $f(\gamma) = 0$, so: 
  \[\displaystyle
        0 = f(\gamma) = a_{0} 1_{X_{0}}(\gamma) + a_{1} 1_{X_{1}}(\gamma) = a_{0} + a_{1} \ ,
\]
so $a_{1} = -a_{0} \not= 0$, and the lemma is proven.
\end{proof} 

\begin{cor}
\label{lem:2hgenerators}
    The ideal $\SSS_{K}(\GGG_{2S})$ of singular functions in the two-headed snake is generated by $1_{X_{0}} - 1_{X_{1}}$.  That is:  
    \[\mathcal{S}_K(\mathcal{G}_{2 S}) = \langle 1_{X_{0}} - 1_{X_{1}} \rangle. \] 
\end{cor}
\begin{proof}
By \cref{singfuncform}, $\mathcal{S}_K(\mathcal{G}_{2 S}) \subseteq \langle 1_{X_0} - 1_{X_1} \rangle$. The reverse containment is clear.  
\end{proof} 


\begin{prop} \label{prop:2hideals}
    The ideal $\SSS_{K}(\GGG_{2S})$ is always $S$-simple; that is, it has no proper non-trivial subsets that are ideals of $\AAA_K(\GGG_{S})$.
\end{prop}
\begin{proof}
 Let $J \subseteq \mathcal{S}_{K}(\mathcal{G}_{2 S})$ be a non-zero ideal of $\AAA_K(\mathcal{G}_{2 S})$. From \cref{lem:2hgenerators}, it suffices to prove that $1_{X_{0}} - 1_{X_{1}}$ belongs to $J$.

    Choose a non-zero $f \in J$. Then $f \in \mathcal{S}_K(\mathcal{G}_{2 S})$, so by \cref{singfuncform}:  
    \[
    f = f(0)(1_{X_0} - 1_{X_1}) \in J \ \text{ with } f(0) \not= 0 \ .
    \] 
  Since $K$ is a field and $J$ is closed under scalar multiplcation, the result follows.   
\end{proof}
\begin{remark}
Note that the choice of the field $K$ (and, in particular, its characteristic) has no effect on the $S$-simplicity of $\SSS_{K}(\GGG_{2S})$. This is not the case for snake groupoids with more heads.
\end{remark}

\section{Singular ideals in the Steinberg algebra of the Three-Headed Snake} 
\subsection{The Three-Headed snake}
%We aim in the future to generalise the two-headed snake groupoid to an arbitrary number of heads, beginning here with the three-headed snake. 
