    The structure of the three-headed snake groupoid is similar to that of the two-headed snake except that we have a copy of $\mathbb{Z} / 3 \mathbb{Z} $ at the heads, instead of $\mathbb{Z} / 2 \mathbb{Z} $. Basic open sets that contain head 2 are cylinder sets that contain 0 with the 0 removed and 2 added.  Then $\mathcal{G}_{3 S}$ is an ample groupoid such that any distinct pair of heads cannot be separated by open sets: thus, this groupoid is again non-Hausdorff with a Hausdorff unit space.

As in the case of the two-headed snake, any set that contains more than one head cannot be a bisection.  Here, singular functions have support contained in the set $\{0,1,2\}$, and a similar argument as in the proof of \cref{2h_formoffunc} shows that each $f \in \AAA_K(\mathcal{G}_{3 S})$ may be written in the form:
\begin{equation} \label{3h_formoffunc_general}
    f = a_{0} 1_{X_{0}} + a_{1} 1_{X_{1}} + a_{2} 1_{X_{2}} + \sum\limits_{B \in F_{w}} a_{B} 1_{B} \ .
\end{equation} 

We introduce the following notation to have a more concise expression for Equation (\ref{3h_formoffunc_general}). Given $a_{0},a_{1},a_{2} \in K$, we let:
\begin{equation} 
\label{def:bracketnotation}
[a_{0},a_{1},a_{2}] := a_{0} 1_{X_{0}} + a_{1} 1_{X_{1}} + a_{2} 1_{X_{2}} .
\end{equation}
Then Equation (\ref{3h_formoffunc_general}) can be written as:
\[ \displaystyle f = [a_{0},a_{1},a_{2}] + \sum\limits_{B \in F_{w}} a_{B} 1_{B} .
\]
In particular, note that every singular function can be written in the form of Equation \eqref{def:bracketnotation}. However, not every equation in this form is necessarily singular -- see \cref{lem:3hsing} below. 

\begin{remark}
    Our analysis of singular functions in $\AAA_K(\mathcal{G}_{3S})$ comes down to analysing certain ideals in the group algebra associated to the group of heads $\Z/3\Z$. Our notation $[a_0,a_1,a_2]$ signals this, and can be formalized, but we do not focus on this aspect. Our approach more naturally lends itself to our longer-term goal of applying similar techniques to more general (non-Hausdorff, ample) groupoids (such as snakes with additional heads and tails, or additional bodies as in \cite[Example~3.5]{CEP}, and so forth)\footnote{We thank Astrid an Huef for pointing this out and for useful related discussions.}.
\end{remark}

Let $f = [a_{0},a_{1},a_{2}] + \sum\limits_{B \in F_{w}} a_{B} 1_{B}$ and $g = [b_{0},b_{1},b_{2}] + \sum\limits_{B' \in F'_{w}} b_{B'} 1_{B'}$ be two elements of the Steinberg algebra $\AAA_K(\GGG_{3S})$. Then the convolution of $f$ and $g$ is given by: 
\begin{equation}
\label{conv}
 \forall \ \gamma \in \GGG_{3S} , \   (f*g)(\gamma) = \begin{cases}
        f(\gamma) \cdot g(\gamma) \text{ if } \gamma \in X \backslash \{0\} \\ 
        a_0 b_0 + a_1 b_2 + a_2 b_1 \text{ if } \gamma = 0 \\ 
        a_2 b_2 + a_0 b_1 + a_1 b_0 \text{ if } \gamma = 1\\ 
        a_1 b_1 + a_0 b_2 + a_2 b_0 \text{ if } \gamma = 2 \\ 
    \end{cases} \ .
\end{equation} 
Note that as multiplication in $K$ is commutative, so is convolution in $\AAA_K(\GGG_{3S})$.

