\subsection{Enter number theory} 
According to \cref{cor:3h_SingIdeals}, the question of whether $\AAA_K(\mathcal{G}_{3 S})$ has singular ideals $\langle [1,b,-(b+1)] \rangle$,  is now a question of whether $K$ contains roots $b$ of the polynomial $T^{2} + T + 1$.

\begin{remark}
    Note that the polynomial $\Phi_{3}(T) := T^{2} + T + 1$ has degree $2$, so if $K$ contains one root of $\Phi_{3}(T)$ then it contains both roots. That is, if $K$ is a rupture field for $\Phi_3(T)$, then $K$ is a splitting field for $\Phi_3(T)$. 
\end{remark}

\begin{prop} \label{3h_nonsimpleiffroots}
    The ideal $\mathcal{S}_{K}(\mathcal{G}_{3 S})$ is not $S$-simple if and only if $K$ is a splitting field for $T^{2} + T + 1$. 
\end{prop}

\begin{proof} 
Recall that $b \in K$ is a root of $\Phi_{3}(T)$ exactly means that $\Delta_{b} = 0_{K}$. That is, by \cref{cor:3h_SingIdeals}, that $\langle [1, b, -(b+1)] \rangle$ is a singular ideal of $\AAA_K(\GGG_{3S})$; thus the proof is complete.
\end{proof} 
