\begin{abstract}
In dynamic graphs, preserving temporal continuity is critical. However, Memory-based Dynamic Graph Neural Networks (MDGNNs) trained with large batches often disrupt event sequences, leading to temporal information loss. This discontinuity not only deteriorates temporal modeling but also hinders optimization by increasing the difficulty of parameter convergence. Our theoretical study quantifies this through a Lipschitz upper bound, showing that large batch sizes enlarge the parameter search space. In response, we propose BADGNN, a novel batch-agnostic framework consisting of two core components: (1) Temporal Lipschitz Regularization (TLR) to control parameter search space expansion, and (2) Adaptive Attention Adjustment (A3) to alleviate attention distortion induced by both regularization and batching. Empirical results on three benchmark datasets show that BADGNN maintains strong performance while enabling significantly larger batch sizes and faster training compared to TGN.
% In Memory-based Dynamic Graph Neural Networks (MDGNNs), temporal discontinuity caused by large-batch training disrupts the sequential order of events, leading to the loss of temporal information. Our analysis shows that this information loss affects the model’s ability to effectively optimize its parameters, making convergence more difficult. Furthermore, we derive an upper Lipschitz bound revealing that larger batch sizes expand the model’s weight search space, which increases the difficulty of finding the optimal parameters. Guided by this insight, we propose Batch-Agnostic Dynamic GNN (BADGNN), which involves two methods: (1) Temporal Lipschitz Regularization (TLR), which restricts the expansion of the weight search space and stabilizes optimization under large batch sizes; and (2) Adaptive Attention Adjustment (A3), which complements TLR by refining attention distributions to mitigate information loss caused by both regularization and large batches. Together, these methods enable BADGNN to robust performance during large-scale training. Extensive experiments on three real-world datasets show that BADGNN supports up to 11× larger batch sizes and achieves 2× training speedup over TGN, without sacrificing performance. 
Our code is available at Code: \url{https://anonymous.4open.science/r/TGN_Lipichitz-C033/} 

\end{abstract}

