	\begin{abstract}
		Quadratic bosonic Hamiltonians and their associated unitary transformations form a fundamental class of operations in quantum optics, modeling key processes such as squeezing, displacement, and beam-splitting. Their Heisenberg-picture dynamics simplifies to linear (or possibly affine) transformations on quadrature operators, enabling efficient analysis and decomposition into optical gate sets using matrix operations. However, this formalism discards a phase, which, while often neglected, is essential for a complete unitary characterization.  
		We present efficient methods to recover this phase directly from the vacuum-to-vacuum amplitude of the unitary, using calculations that scale polynomially with the number of modes and avoid Fock space manipulations. We reduce to integration the general problem, including the case of time-dependent Hamiltonians, and provide analytical results for key cases including when the Hamiltonian is positive definite, passive, active, or single-mode.
	\end{abstract}
	\maketitle


	\section{Introduction}
	Quadratic bosonic Hamiltonians and their associated Gaussian unitaries are among the most widely studied operations in quantum optics~\cite{serafini2023quantum}. They describe fundamental processes such as mode squeezing and displacement, as well as beam splitting, phase and controlled-phase operations in the continuous-variable model of quantum computation~\cite{lloyd1999quantum} and certain discrete-variable quantum error correction encodings~\cite{gottesman2001encoding}. These transformations can be efficiently analyzed in the Heisenberg picture, where their action on the quadrature operators reduces to linear (or possibly affine) transformations implemented via symplectic matrices (and possibly displacement vectors).

	This symplectic formalism enables the decomposition of such transformations into optical gate sets—e.g., beam splitters, squeezers, and displacements—through purely finite-dimensional matrix operations~\cite{braunstein2005squeezing,Reck1994decomposition,Clements2016decomposition,de2018simple,kumar2021unitary,zhou2024bosehedral,LpezPastor2021,bell2021further,arends2024decomposing,yasir2025compactifying,pereira2020minimum,saygin2020robust,idel2015sinkhorn}. However, a key limitation of this approach is the loss of phase information: two unitaries that act identically on quadratures may still differ by a physically meaningful phase. One can use  Schur’s lemma(cf. Appendices A and B  of Ballentine~\cite{ballentine2014quantum}) to formalize this observation by stating that such unitaries differ by at most a phase when their action is indistinguishable on an irreducible set of operators, such as the bosonic quadratures.

	This phase, although often neglected, plays an essential role in several contexts: it appears in superpositions of Gaussian states~\cite{yao2024riemannian,marshall2023simulation,bourassa2021fast,tara1993production} and in controlled-unitary operations relevant to relativistic quantum information~\cite{sachs2017entanglement,funai2025gaussian}, and it is also important for the development of quantum information technologies~\cite{didier2015fast,zhu2013circuit}. Furthermore, accurate phase accounting is necessary when Gaussian unitaries are combined into larger computations or used as components in approximating non-Gaussian states and processes~\cite{yao2024riemannian,motamedi2025stellar,dias2024classical}. 

    We should mention that related results have been very recently derived by Guaita et al.~\cite{guaita2024representation} using tools from representation theory for time-independent (fermionic and bosonic) Hamiltonians. Similarly, Funai~\cite{funai2025gaussian} has addressed related problems in the context of quantum fields for a set of restricted Hamiltonians in quantum field theory. Our results address the problem using linear algebra tools and generalize to time-dependent problems.

	In this work, we present efficient methods to recover the phase of quadratic unitaries by computing their vacuum-to-vacuum amplitude. These methods scale polynomially with the number of modes and entirely avoid manipulations in the infinite-dimensional Fock space. We provide analytical results for special cases
	---when the Hamiltonian is positive definite, passive, active, or single-mode--- and 
	 show how the general multimode problem can be reduced to integration.


	The manuscript is structured as follows. Sec.~\ref{sec:problem} formalizes the problem and sets up the notation. Sec.~\ref{sec:Hpos} presents an analytical method based on the Williamson decomposition for the case of positive-definite Hamiltonian matrices. The general case is addressed in Sec.~\ref{sec:general}, where we show how the vacuum amplitude can be obtained via a reduction to linear differential equations. Analytical results are provided for key limiting cases, and concluding remarks, including a discussion of time-dependent problems, follow in Sec.~\ref{sec:final}.



	\section{Notation and Problem statement }\label{sec:problem}
	We setup some basic notation before stating the problem. The vector of quadrature operators of an $M$-mode system is~\cite{serafini2023quantum}
	\begin{align}
		\bm{\hat r} &= 
		\hat{\bm{x}}  \oplus 
		\hat{\bm{p}}
		= (\hat x_1,\ldots,\hat x_M, \hat p_1,\ldots,\hat p_M)^T.
	\end{align}
	These operators satisfy the canonical commutation relations $[\hat{r}_j,\hat{r}_k] = i \hbar \Omega_{jk}$ where $	
		\bm{\Omega} = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}
			0 & \ \mathbb{I}_M \\
			-\mathbb{I}_M & \ 0
	\end{smallmatrix}\right),$
	is the so-called symplectic matrix, and $\mathbb{I}_M$ is the $M$-dimensional identity matrix.
	It is also useful to introduce annihilation and creation (collectively ladder) operators for mode $j$
	\begin{align}
		\ha_j = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2 \hbar}}(\hat{x}_j+ i \hat{p}_j), \quad \had_j =  \frac{1}{\sqrt{2 \hbar}}(\hat{x}_j- i \hat{p}_j).
	\end{align}
	Finally, the multimode vacuum state $\ket{\bm{0}} = \ket{0}_1 \otimes \ldots \otimes \ket{0}_M$ as the state that satisfies $\ha_j \ket{\bm{0}} = 0 \ \forall j$. Note that we use boldface for vectors and matrices and use hats for operators in Fock space.

	The question that concerns this manuscript is motivated by the following problem: given a quadratic Hamiltonian in quadrature operators~\cite{serafini2023quantum}, specified by an even-dimensional symmetric matrix $\bm{H}$ (the case where the Hamiltonian has linear terms in the quadrature operators is analyzed in Appendix \ref{app:linear}), 
	\begin{align}
		\HH = \tfrac{1}{2\hbar} \hbr^T \bm{H} \hbr = \tfrac{1}{2\hbar} \sum_{k=1}^M \sum_{l = 1}^M H_{kl} \hat{r}_k \hat{r}_l ,
	\end{align}
	how can one factor its associated unitary operator 
	\begin{align}\label{eq:unit}
		\uu = \exp(- i\hat{\mathcal{H}}t)=\exp\left(- \tfrac{i}{2 \hbar} \hbr^T \bm{H}  \hbr t \right)
	\end{align}
	into simple one- and two-mode gates, such as squeezing and beamsplitter operations?

	Significant progress on this problem can be obtained by looking at the action of the operator in Eq.~\eqref{eq:unit} in the Heisenberg picture. Defining 
	\begin{align}\label{eq:heisenberg}
		\hbr(t) &= \uu  \hbr \uu^\dagger\\
		\frac{d}{dt} \hbr(t) &= \bm{\Omega} \bm{H} \hbr(t) \longrightarrow \hbr(t) = \underbrace{\exp(\bm{\Omega} \bm{H} t)}_{\bm{S}} \bm{r}.
	\end{align}
	It is straightforward to verify that the matrix $\bm{S}$ is symplectic, satisfying $\bm{S} \bm{\Omega} \bm{S}^T = \bm{\Omega}$.

	One may invoke Schur's lemma (cf. Appendices A and B of Ballantine~\cite{ballentine2014quantum}) to argue that any sequence of unitary operations $\hat{\mathcal{V}}$ that achieves
	$\hat{\mathcal{V}} \bm{r} \hat{\mathcal{V}}^\dagger = \bm{S} \bm{r}$ must be equal to $\uu$ up to a phase. Typically, this sequence is obtained by combining passive operations, which do not change the total photon number, and active operations in the form of squeezing\footnote{The precise way to obtain this sequence using the Bloch-Messiah decomposition of the symplectic matrix associated with the unitary transformation $\uu$ is discussed in Sec. 4.1 of  Ref.~\cite{kalajdzievski2021exact}. A self contained discussion of how to obtain the Bloch-Messiah decomposition of a symplectic matrix can be found in Ref.~\cite{houde2024matrix}.}. The method just described is successful at finding a suitable sequence of gates to decompose the action of a quadratic Hamiltonian but is of course unable to account for the phase in the argument just presented. This motivates the main problem addressed by this manuscript, how to obtain this phase which cannot be accounted for once one considers evolution in the Heisenberg picture Eq.~\eqref{eq:heisenberg}.

	To give an example of when this phase is non-trivial consider the single-mode quadratic-phase gate
	\begin{align}
		\hat{P}(t) = \exp(i t \hat{x}^2  / 2\hbar),
	\end{align}
from which we read $\bm{H} = -\left(\begin{smallmatrix}
	1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0    
\end{smallmatrix} \right)$ and find
\begin{align}
\bm{S} &= \exp(\bm{\Omega} \bm{H} t) = \begin{pmatrix}
	1 & 0 \\
	t & 1
\end{pmatrix}\\
&= \begin{pmatrix}
	\cos \theta &-\sin \theta \\
	\sin \theta & \cos \theta
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
	e^{r} & 0 \\
	0 & e^{-r}
\end{pmatrix}
\begin{pmatrix}
	\cos \tilde \theta &-\sin \tilde\theta \\
	\sin \tilde \theta & \cos \tilde \theta
\end{pmatrix},
\end{align}
where the parameters above are related to $t$ as follows  $\sinh r = t/2, \  \theta = \atan2(e^{r},1), \ \tilde{\theta} = \tfrac{\pi}{2}-\theta$ (cf. Eq. 41 of Ref.~\cite{kalajdzievski2021exact}).
The Bloch-Messiah decomposition above gives the following sequence in Fock space
	\begin{align}\label{eq:put}
		\hat{\mathcal{V}} &= \exp\left( i \theta \had \ha \right) \exp(\tfrac{r}{2}(\ha^{\dagger 2} - \ha^2)) \exp\left( i \tilde{\theta} \had \ha  \right).
	\end{align}

It is easy to see that the equation above does not correctly capture the phase of the gate as
	\begin{align}
		\braket{0|\hat{\mathcal{V}}|0} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\cosh r}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt[4]{1+\tfrac{t^2}{4}}} \in \mathbb{R}^+,
	\end{align}
	while for the actual gate one can use that $\braket{0|x} = \frac{1}{\sqrt[4]{\pi \hbar}} \exp(-x^2 / (2 \hbar))$ and that the (single-mode) identity operator can be resolved in terms of eigenkets of the position operator $\mathbb{1} = \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} dx \ket{x}\bra{x}$ to find 
	\begin{align}
		\braket{0|\hat{P}(t)|0} &= \int_{-\infty}^{\infty} dx \braket{0|P(t)|x} \braket{x|0}\\
		&=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} dx \braket{0|x} \exp\left( i\frac{t}{2 \hbar} x^2 \right) \braket{x|0} \\
		&=\int_{-\infty}^{\infty} dx  \exp\left( i\frac{t}{2 \hbar} x^2 \right) |\braket{x|0}|^2 \\
		&= \frac{1}{\sqrt{1 - i \tfrac{t}{2}}} \in \mathbb{C}.
	\end{align}
	We see that the phase we are after is also the phase associated with the vacuum-to-vacuum amplitude of the unitary in Eq.~\eqref{eq:unit}, and we can write $\sqrt[4]{1+\tfrac{t^2}{4}}/\sqrt{1 - i \tfrac{t}{2}}$ as the correction to the decomposition in Eq.~\eqref{eq:put}.

	In the rest of the manuscript we show how the amplitude and phase
	\begin{align}
		\alpha = \braket{\bm{0}| \exp\left(- \tfrac{i}{2\hbar} \hbr \bm{H}  \hbr t \right)| \bm{0}}, \  e^{i\varphi} = \alpha/|\alpha|,
	\end{align}
	can be obtained.
	Note that the absolute value of this  amplitude, the vacuum-to-vacuum \emph{probability}, is easy to find. To see this simply note 
	\begin{align}
		|\alpha|^2 =  \bra{\bm{0}} \uu \ket{\bm{0}}  \bra{\bm{0}} \uu ^\dagger \ket{\bm{0}}.
	\end{align}
	This can be interpreted as the fidelity between two pure Gaussian states, namely $\uu \ket{\bm{0}}  \bra{\bm{0}} \uu ^\dagger$ and $ \ket{\bm{0}}  \bra{\bm{0}}$, for which we can use phase-space methods to obtain the probability~\cite{brask2021gaussian}
	\begin{align}\label{eq:vacprob}
		|\alpha|^2 = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\det\left[ \frac{1}{2} (\bm{S} \bm{S}^T + \mathbb{I}_{2M}) \right]}},
	\end{align}
	which is also proportional to the value of the Husimi Q-function of the Gaussian state $\uu \ket{\bm{0}}  \bra{\bm{0}} \uu ^\dagger$ with Husimi covariance matrix $\frac{1}{2} (\bm{S} \bm{S}^T + \mathbb{I}_{2M} ) $ evaluated at the origin.

	Finally, note that with the tools we develop below one can also calculate directly the vacuum-to-vacuum amplitude of any Gaussian state, which is obtained from the vacuum by application of a Gaussian unitary $\ket{\psi} = \hat{\mathcal{Y}} \ket{\bm{0}}$ since we can always do
	\begin{align}
	\bra{\psi}\uu\ket{\psi} &=   \bra{\bm{0}}\hat{\mathcal{Y}}^\dagger \exp\left(- \tfrac{i}{2 \hbar} \hbr^T \bm{H}  \hbr t \right) \hat{\mathcal{Y}} \ket{\bm{0}} \\
	 &=\bra{\bm{0}} \exp\left(- \tfrac{i}{2 \hbar} \hat{\mathcal{Y}}^\dagger \hbr^T \bm{H}  \hbr  \hat{\mathcal{Y}}  t \right)\ket{\bm{0}}\\
	 &=\bra{\bm{0}} \exp\left(- \tfrac{i}{2 \hbar}  \hbr^T \underbrace{ \bm{M}^T \bm{H}  \bm{M} }_{\equiv \bm{H'}} \hbr  t \right)\ket{\bm{0}}
	\end{align}
where we used that $ \hat{\mathcal{Y}}^\dagger \hbr  \hat{\mathcal{Y}} = \bm{M} \hbr  $ with $\bm{M}$ a symplectic matrix. Thus the calculation of the amplitude for an arbitrary Gaussian state reduces to the one of the vacuum-to-vacuum amplitude of a different Hamiltonian.




	\section{Positive definite Hamiltonians}\label{sec:Hpos}
	If $\bm{H}$ is positive definite\footnote{or negative definite in which case we can use $\bm{H }t = -t (-\bm{H})$} we can use Williamson's theorem~\cite{williamson1937normal,houde2024matrix} to write
\begin{align}
	\bm{H} = \bm{T}^T (\bm{d} \oplus \bm{d}) \bm{T} 
\end{align}
where $\bm{T}$ is a symplectic matrix and $\bm{d}$ is diagonal matrix of size $M$ with nonnegative diagonal entries $d_i$.
The symplectic matrix can be obtained by acting a certain Gaussian unitary $ e^{i \theta} \hat{\mathcal{T}}$ that satisfies
\begin{align}
	\hat{\mathcal{T}}^\dagger \hat{\bm{r}} \hat{\mathcal{T}} =\bm{T} \hat{\bm{r}} \rightarrow  \tfrac{1}{2 \hbar}  \hat{\bm{r}}^T \bm{T}^T (\bm{d} \oplus \bm{d}) \bm{T} \hat{\bm{r}} = \hat{\mathcal{T}}^\dagger   \hbr^T \left[\tfrac{\bm{d}\oplus \bm{d}}{2 \hbar } \right] \hbr  \hat{\mathcal{T}}
\end{align}
we can now write $\tfrac{1}{2 \hbar } \hbr^T [\bm{d}\oplus \bm{d}] \hbr  = \sum_{i=1}^M \tfrac{d_i}{2\hbar} (\hat{x}_i^2 + \hat{p}_i^2) =  \sum_{i=1}^M d_i (\hat{n}_i + \tfrac12) $ with $\hat{n}_i = \hat{a}_i^\dagger \hat{a}_i$ the number operator of mode $i$. This allows us to write
\begin{align}
	\exp\left(-i \hat{\mathcal{H}}t  \right) %
	=& e^{-i t \sum_{j=1}^M d_j/2} \hat{\mathcal{T}}^\dagger  \exp\left(-i  t\sum_{j=1}^M d_j  \hat{n}_j    \right)\hat{\mathcal{T}} ,\label{eq:HP}
\end{align}
Introducing the notation $\ket{\bm{n}} = \ket{n_1}\otimes \ldots \otimes \ket{n_M}$ for a multimode Fock state, a joint eigenstate of all the number operators, we can write
\begin{align}
\exp\left(-i \sum_{j=1}^M d_j t \hat{n}_j    \right) = \sum_{\bm{n} \in \mathbb{N}_0^M} e^{-i \bm{n} \cdot \bm{d} t} \ket{\bm{n}} \bra{\bm{n}}.
\end{align}
It follows that, using the formula for the photon-number moment generating function of Gaussian state (cf. Eq.~65 of Bulmer et al.~\cite{bulmer2024simulating} or Eq.~30 of Cardin et al.~\cite{cardin2024photon}) we obtain
\begin{align}
	\alpha =& \braket{\bm{0}|\exp\left(-\frac{i}{2 \hbar} \hat{\bm{r}}^T \bm{H} \hat{\bm{r}} t\right) |\bm{0}} \\
	=& e^{-i \sum_{j=1}^M d_j t/2}\sum_{\bm{n} \in \mathbb{N}_0^M} e^{-i \bm{n} \cdot \bm{d} t} \braket{\bm{0}|\hat{\mathcal{T}}^\dagger|\bm{n}} \braket{\bm{n}|\hat{\mathcal{T}}|\bm{0}} \\
	=&e^{-i \sum_{j=1}^Md_j t/2}\frac{1}{\sqrt{\det\left( \mathbb{I}_{2M} -  \bm{K}(\bm{d}) \left[\bm{T} \bm{T}^T - \mathbb{I}_{2M}  \right]/2\right)}}, \label{eq:will}
\end{align}
where $\bm{K}(\bm{d}) = [\oplus_{j=1}^M (e^{-i d_j t}-1)] \oplus [\oplus_{j=1}^M (e^{-i d_j t}-1)]$. %
To arrive to Eq.~\eqref{eq:will} we used that the covariance matrix of the Glauber-Sudarshan P function of the Gaussian state $\hat{\mathcal{T}} \ket{\bm{0}} \bra{\bm{0}} \hat{\mathcal{T}}^\dagger$ is precisely $\tfrac12 (\bm{T} \bm{T}^T - \mathbb{I}_{2M})$.


	Note that the case where $\bm{H}$ is positive semi-definite can be handled as a limit (and that there exists generalizations of Williamson's theorem~\cite{mishra2024generalization}). For example, we can obtain the vacuum-to-vacuum amplitude of the phase gate $\hat{P}(-t)$, as the limit as $\epsilon \to 0$ of $
	\bm{H} = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}
		1 & 0 \\ 0 & 0    
	\end{smallmatrix} \right) + \epsilon \mathbb{I}_2$.
	We can write the Williamson decomposition of $\bm{H}$ as
	\begin{align}
		\bm{d} = [\sqrt{\epsilon(1+\epsilon)}], \ 
		\bm{T} = \left( \begin{smallmatrix}
			z & 0 \\
			0 & \tfrac{1}{z}
		\end{smallmatrix} \right), \ z=\sqrt[4]{\tfrac{\epsilon}{1+\epsilon}},
	\end{align}
	for which we can use Eq.~\eqref{eq:will} to obtain
	\begin{align}
		\alpha = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\cos t \sqrt{\epsilon(1+\epsilon)} + i t(\tfrac12+ \epsilon)  \sinc t  \sqrt{\epsilon(1+\epsilon)} }},
	\end{align}
	which indeed reduces to $1/\sqrt{1+i t/2}$ as $\epsilon \to 0$.

	To conclude this section we note that any factorization of the form $\bm{H} = \bm{T}^T [\bm{e} \oplus \bm{f}] \bm{T}$ with $\bm{T}$ symplectic and $\bm{e}$ and $\bm{f}$ real and diagonal will allow us to find the amplitude as we can write
	\begin{align}
	&\exp( \tfrac{i}{2\hbar} \hbr^T \bm{T} [\bm{e} \oplus \bm{f}] \bm{T}^T \hbr ) \\ &=\hat{\mathcal{T}}^\dagger \bigotimes_{j=1}^M\exp\left(\tfrac{i}{2 \hbar} [e_j \hat{x}_j^2 + f_j \hat{p}_j^2] \right) \hat{\mathcal{T}}, \nonumber
	\end{align}
	Where now the terms inside the product are single-mode Hamiltonians for which we provide a simple formula for the computation of their phase in the next section. Once this phase is known one can find the phase of the net unitary by using the Bargmann formalism for composing Gaussian unitaries Ref.~\cite{yao2024riemannian} or the triple Gaussian integral methods from Refs.~\cite{dias2024classical,paraoanu2000fidelity}.

	\section{General Hamiltonians}\label{sec:general}
	To express the solution for an arbitrary Hamiltonian we would like to express $\hat{\mathcal{H}}$ in terms of ladder operators. After some algebra we find 
	\begin{align}
		\hat{\mathcal{H}} =&\tfrac14 \tr \bm{H} + :\hat{\mathcal{H}}:\\
		:\hat{\mathcal{H}}: =%
		& \tfrac{1}{4} \sum_{i,j=1}^M \underbrace{[E_{ij}-G_{ij} - i (F_{ij}+F_{ji})]}_{\equiv 4f_{ij}} a_i^\dagger a_j^\dagger + \text{H.c.}\\
		&+ \tfrac{1}{2} \sum_{i,j=1}^M \underbrace{[E_{ij}+G_{ij} + i (F_{ji}-F_{ij})]}_{\equiv 2 \omega_{ij}} a_i^\dagger a_j,
	\end{align}
	where we wrote $\bm{H} = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}
		\bm{E} & \bm{F} \\ \bm{F}^T & \bm{G}
	\end{smallmatrix} \right)$, with $\bm{E} = \bm{E}^T$, $\bm{G} = \bm{G}^T$ and $\bm{f} = \bm{f}^T$, $\bm{\omega} = \bm{\omega}^\dagger$ and introduced the normal-ordered operator $:\hat{\mathcal{H}}:$.
	We can then write
	\begin{align}\label{eq:ladder}
		\hat{\mathcal{H}}=  \tfrac{1}{4} \text{tr}\bm{H} + \sum_{ij} \omega_{ij}a_i^\dagger a_j  +\sum_{ij} \left( f_{ij} a_i^\dagger a_j^\dagger  + \text{H.c.} \right).
	\end{align}


	Having set up this notation, we can very generally calculate the vacuum-to-vacuum amplitude as\footnote{In Ref.~\cite{ma1990multimode} Ma and Rhodes show how to obtain a normal ordered expression for Hamiltonians of the form
		\begin{align}
			\hat{H}_N(t) =& \sum_{ij=1}^M \omega_{ij}(t) \ha_i^\dagger  \ha_j + \sum_{ij=1}^M f_{ij}(t) \ha^\dagger_i \ha_j^\dagger +  \sum_{ij=1}^M f_{ij}^*(t) \ha_i \ha_j\\
			&+\sum_{i=1}^M [ g_i(t) \had + g_i^*(t) \ha] + h(t) .
		\end{align}
		In their Eq. 4.8 they write a normal-ordered expression for the unitary evolution operator of the form
		\begin{align}
			U_N(t) =& \exp(A(t)) \exp\left[\sum_{i=1}^M B_i(t) \had_i + \sum_{ij=1}^M C_{ij}\had_i \had_j \right] \\
			&:\exp\left(\sum_{ij=1}^M D_{ij}(t) \had_i \ha_j \right): \exp\left[\sum_{i=1}^M E_i(t) \ha_i + \sum_{ij=1}^M F_{ij}\ha_i \ha_j \right] .
		\end{align}
		Since, as we show in the Appendix \ref{app:linear}, all linear terms can be removed from the Hamiltonian we can take without loss of generality $g_{i} = 0=E_i=B_i$.
		Then the quantity $\exp(A(t))$ is precisely $\alpha$ and upon eliminating all linear terms, Eq.~\eqref{eq:ricatti} is the equation of motion for $\bm{C}$.
	}
	\begin{align}\label{eq:phase}
		\alpha = \exp\left[-i \int_0^t dt' \left(  \tfrac{1}{4} \tr \bm{H}  + 2 \tr(\bm{f}^\dagger \bm{C}(t')) \right)\right],
	\end{align}
	where $\bm{C}(t)$ satisfies the Riccati equation 
	\begin{align}\label{eq:ricatti}
		i \frac{d}{dt} \bm{C}(t) = 4 \bm{C}(t) \bm{f}^\dagger \bm{C}(t) + 2 \bm{\omega} \bm{C}(t) + \bm{f}.
	\end{align}
with initial value $\bm{C}(0)=0$.
	We can write this equation in a more convenient way by introducing $\bm{X}(t) = 2\bm{C}(t)$ to find
	\begin{align}
		\frac{d}{dt} \bm{X}(t) = -i 2 \bm{X}(t) \bm{f}^\dagger \bm{X}(t) -2 i  \bm{\omega} \bm{X}(t) - 2i\bm{f},
	\end{align} 
	This matrix-valued nonlinear differential equation can be solved using Radon's lemma (cf. Theorem 3.1.1. of Ref.~\cite{abou2012matrix}). First we form the linear system
	\begin{align}\label{eq:asslinear}
		\frac{d}{dt} \begin{pmatrix} \bm{R}(t) \\ \bm{S}(t)\end{pmatrix} &= i \underbrace{\begin{pmatrix} \bm{0} &  2 \bm{f}^\dagger \\
				-2  \bm{f} & -2  \bm{\omega} \end{pmatrix}}_{\equiv \bm{\Lambda}}  \begin{pmatrix} \bm{R}(t) \\ \bm{S}(t)\end{pmatrix} ,
	\end{align}
with initial conditions	$\bm{R}(0) = \mathbb{I}_M$ and $\bm{S}(0)=0$ (giving $\bm{C}(0) = \bm{X}(0)=0$). The solution of  this linear system can now be written as $		\left(\begin{smallmatrix} \bm{R}(t) \\ \bm{S}(t)\end{smallmatrix} \right) = \exp\left[ i  \bm{\Lambda}t  \right] \left( \begin{smallmatrix} \mathbb{I}_M \\ \bm{0}_M \end{smallmatrix}\right),$
and from it we obtain $\bm{X}(t) = \bm{S}(t) \bm{R}^{-1}(t)$ and the amplitude
	\begin{align}\label{eq:quad}
		\alpha = \exp\left[   -i \int_0^t dt' \left\{ \tfrac{1}{4} \tr \bm{H}  +\tr[\bm{f}^\dagger \bm{S}(t') \bm{R}^{-1}(t')] \right\} \right].
	\end{align}


	Two special sets of Hamiltonians can be defined by looking at the expression of $\hat{\mathcal{H}}$ in terms of ladder operators Eq.~\eqref{eq:ladder}. These correspond to the cases
	\begin{align}
		\bm{\omega} =0 &\leftrightarrow \bm{E} = -\bm{G}, \ \bm{F} = \bm{F}^T \text{ active} ,\\
		\bm{f} =0 &\leftrightarrow \bm{E} = \bm{G}, \ \bm{F} = -\bm{F}^T \text{  passive}.
	\end{align}
	For the  passive case $\bm{f} = 0$ it readily follows from Eq.~\eqref{eq:quad} that $\alpha = \exp(- i t \tr \bm{H}/4)$; this of course is not surprising since, for passive hamiltonians, $\ket{\bm{0}}$ is an eigenket of $:\hat{\mathcal{H}}:$ with eigenvalue 0.

	For the active case $\bm{\omega}=0$ (note that this implies that $\tr \bm{H}=0$) we can introduce the Autonne-Takagi~\cite{houde2024matrix} decomposition of the symmetric matrix $2\bm{f} =  \bm{W} \bm{s} \bm{W}^T$ with $\bm{s}$ diagonal and $\bm{W}$ unitary which allow us to write
	\begin{align}
		& 2  i t    \begin{pmatrix} \bm{0} &  \bm{f}^\dagger \\
			- \bm{f} &  \bm{0} \end{pmatrix}  = \begin{pmatrix}
			\bm{W}^* & \bm{0} \\
			\bm{0} & \bm{W}
		\end{pmatrix}
		\begin{pmatrix}
			\bm{0} & i t\bm{s} \\
			- i t\bm{s} & \bm{0}
		\end{pmatrix}
		\begin{pmatrix}
			\bm{W}^T & \bm{0} \\
			\bm{0} & \bm{W}^\dagger
		\end{pmatrix}.
	\end{align}
	We can now use the identity $\exp\left[i t \left(\begin{smallmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ -1 & 0\end{smallmatrix} \right) \right] = \left( \begin{smallmatrix} \cosh t & i \sinh t \\ -i \sinh t & \cosh t\end{smallmatrix} \right)$ and that $\int_0^t dt' \tanh t'  = \ln \cosh t $ to obtain
	\begin{align}
		\alpha = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\prod_{j=1}^M \cosh s_j t}}.
	\end{align}

	We note that one can evaluate the amplitude with relative ease numerically. Assuming that the matrix $\bm{\Lambda}$ can be diagonalized as $\bm{\Lambda} = \bm{U} \bm{\lambda} \bm{U}^{-1}$  with $\bm{\lambda}$ a diagonal matrix then we can immediately compute 
	$\exp(i \bm{\Lambda} t) = \bm{U} \exp( i \bm{\lambda} t) \bm{U}^{-1}$. If the diagonalization of the matrix $\bm{\Lambda}$ is known, calculating $\exp(i \bm{\Lambda} t) $ requires only matrix multiplication, and thus one can evaluate the integrand of Eq.~\eqref{eq:quad}. Once an efficient blackbox exists  for evaluation of the integrand then one can perform the integration with any numerical integration method.



	We conclude this section by considering the general case of a single mode $M=1$. In this case $\bm{f} =f = \tfrac{1}{4}(E-G + 2 i F)$ is a single complex number and $\bm{\omega}=\omega=\tfrac12 (E+G) = \tfrac12 \tr \bm{H}$ is a single real number. It will also be convenient to recall that now $\bm{H} = \left(\begin{smallmatrix}
		E & F \\ F & G
	\end{smallmatrix} \right)$ and that $\det \bm{H} = EG -F^2 = -(\omega^2 -  4 |f|^2)$. With this notation we can obtain after some work\footnote{
	Calculating the $2 \times 2$ matrix exponential $\exp\left(2 i \left[\begin{smallmatrix}
		0 &  f^* \\
		-f & -\omega
 	\end{smallmatrix}  \right] t \right)$ gives 
	\begin{align}
		R(t) =& \mu \left( \cos  t \sqrt{\det \bm{H}}  +\tfrac{i \tr \bm{H}}{2 \sqrt{\det \bm{H}}} \sin t \sqrt{\det \bm{H}} \right) \nonumber ,\\
		S(t)=&-\frac{2 i f \mu   \sin  t\sqrt{\det \bm{H}} t}{\sqrt{\det \bm{H}}}, \ \mu = e^{-\frac{1}{2} i t \tr \bm{H}},
	\end{align} 
	from which the integrand in Eq.~\eqref{eq:quad} is simply
	\begin{align}
		&=\frac{-\sqrt{\det \bm{H}} \tr \bm{H}-2 i \det \bm{H} \tan  t \sqrt{\det \bm{H}} }{-4 i \sqrt{\det \bm{H}} + 2 \tr \bm{H} \tan \sqrt{ t\det \bm{H}} }.
	\end{align}
	and we use
		\begin{align}
			&\int_0^t dt' \frac{a + b \tan y t'}{c+ d \tan y t'} =\frac{t (a c+b d)}{c^2+d^2} - \frac{(b c-a d) \ln [ \cos t y +\tfrac{d}{c} \sin t y]}{y \left(c^2+d^2\right)}.
		\end{align}
	to obtain the equation in the main text.
}
\begin{align}\label{eq:singlemode}
	\alpha = \frac{1}{\sqrt{\cos t \sqrt{\det \bm{H}} + i  t \frac{\tr \bm{H}}{2} \sinc t  \sqrt{\det \bm{H}}}}.
\end{align}
Note that this formula could also have been obtained using the operator ordering theorems in Appendix 5 of Barnett and Radmore~\cite{barnett2002methods}, specifically, Eq. A5.18.



	\section{Final remarks}\label{sec:final}
We have analyzed the vacuum-to-vacuum amplitude associated with unitaries generated by quadratic bosonic Hamiltonians. For Hamiltonians with positive-definite matrices, we derived a closed-form expression for this amplitude by leveraging recent results on the photon-number characteristic function of Gaussian states~\cite{bulmer2024simulating,cardin2024photon}.

	For more general quadratic Hamiltonians, we extended the seminal approach of Ma and Rhodes~\cite{ma1990multimode}, showing that the problem reduces to solving a system of coupled linear differential equations. These equations encode the nonlinear Riccati dynamics governing the amplitude's evolution and can be integrated numerically with polynomial cost in the number of modes. This provides a practical and scalable method for recovering the phase of arbitrary multimode Gaussian unitaries without resorting to infinite-dimensional representations.
	The results presented in Sec.~\ref{sec:general} strongly suggest that most Hamiltonians, other than those that are passive or active\footnote{A notable exception to this general rule are the two-mode controlled-X gate and controlled-phase gate
		\begin{align}
			\hat{\text{C}}_X(t) = \exp\left(-i \tfrac{t}{\hbar} \hat{x}_1 \hat{p}_2 \right) , \quad 			\hat{\text{C}}_Z(t) = \exp\left(i \tfrac{t}{\hbar} \hat{x}_1 \hat{x}_2 \right).
		\end{align}
	The reason why the phase is trivial for these unitaries is that they both can be decomposed as $\hat{\text{C}}_j = \hat{\mathcal{R}}_j [\hat{P}_1(t) \otimes \hat{P}_2(-t) ] \hat{\mathcal{R}}_j^\dagger, \ j \in \{X,Z\}$ with $\hat{P}_i$ a phase-gate in mode $i$ and $\hat{\mathcal{R}}$ a passive unitary. From this we can immediately read that the vacuum-to-vacuum amplitude is $\alpha = 1/\sqrt{1+t^2/4} \in \mathbb{R}^+$.
	}, generate gates with non-trivial phase dependence on $t$. Indeed, for the case of single-mode gates this can be shown by inspecting Eq.~\eqref{eq:singlemode}.

Finally, our approach immediately generalizes to time-dependent Hamiltonians. The differential equation~\eqref{eq:ricatti} is still valid if the Hamiltonian (equivalently $\bm{f}$ and $\bm{\omega}$) is time dependent; it can be solved by trotterization~\cite{trotter1959product,suzuki1976generalized} and used to integrate Eq.~\eqref{eq:phase} and obtain the vacuum-to-vacuum amplitude of time dependent Hamiltonians. Indeed, this is precisely how Heisenberg dynamics (Eq.~\eqref{eq:heisenberg}) is solved-for with time dependent Hamiltonians, an strategy that is heavily employed in the context of quantum nonlinear optics~\cite{quesada2020theory,helt2020degenerate,kopylov2025theory,houde2024perfect}.






