\begin{example}[Inference on linear systems with unknown coefficients]
\label{exampMILSUC}
Consider the setting of Examples \ref{exampMIARP} and \ref{exampMIFSST}, where the coefficient matrix \( A \) is unknown and must be estimated from the data. In such settings, the linear programming characterizations of the null hypotheses in \ref{eqnLPARP} and \ref{eqnLPFSST} remain valid. However, as the feasible regions of these LPs depend on A, the feasible region is now unknown and must be ``estimated'' from the data. 

One way in which such hypotheses may arise is when the researcher is interested in inferring \( \tau_0 = C \theta_0 \), for some known matrix \( C \), where the identified set for \( \theta_0 \) is defined by the linear system of inequalities:
\[
A\theta \leq b
\]
and both the matrix \( A \) and the vector \( b \) may be unknown. Following the suggestion of \cite{FSST}, a confidence interval for the subvector \( \tau_0 \) can be obtained by inverting a test of the following hypothesis for various candidate values of \( \tau \):
\begin{equation}
\label{eqnLSUC}
H_0: \exists\, \theta \text{ such that } A\theta \leq b \quad \text{and} \quad C\theta = \tau.
\end{equation}
It is easy to verify that the latter hypothesis can be written in the form of \ref{eqnMIARP} or \ref{eqnMIFSST}, where the coefficient matrix A is now potentially unknown. Below, we propose a uniformly valid inference procedure for the hypothesis in \ref{eqnMIARP} or \ref{eqnMIFSST} when the matrix \( A \) must be estimated. The conditions required for uniform validity are similar to those in \cite{ARP}, \cite{CS}, and \cite{FSST}\footnote{The main additional assumption we impose is Assumption \ref{assSSLSUC4}, which requires a uniform bound on a "condition number" of the matrices \( A \).}.

When \( C \) is a vector—so that \( \tau_0 \) is scalar—the identified set for \( \tau_0 \) is given by the interval \([v_L, v_U]\), where \( v_L \) and \( v_U \) are the optimal values of the following linear programs:
\begin{equation}
v_L = \min \{ C^\top \theta \mid A\theta \leq b \}, \quad v_U = \max \{ C^\top \theta \mid A\theta \leq b \}.
\end{equation}
Thus, the endpoints of the identified set correspond to the values of two LPs, and constructing a confidence interval for the \textit{identified set} of \( \tau_0 \) reduces to inferring the values \( v_L \) and \( v_U \). We propose such an inference procedure below, which is uniformly valid over a smaller class of DGPs than those required to obtain uniform inference for the null in \ref{eqnLSUC}. Therefore, although the procedure that we propose for inferring \( v_L \) and \( v_U \) provides a computationally cheap way of conducting inference on the identified set of $\tau_0$, it is uniformly valid under more stringent assumptions than are needed to construct uniformly valid confidence intervals for $\tau_0$ through test inversion of the hypothesis in \ref{eqnLSUC}. For empirical applications that motivate such inference procedures, see \cite{BG} and \cite{JHTR}.
\end{example}


