\section{Pointwise valid inference procedure}
\label{sectionPVP}
In this section, we develop a pointwise valid inference procedure for the value of a linear program (LP). Specifically, we consider the estimation of $v$, the value of the LP
\begin{equation}
\label{eqnPW1}
\min\bigl\{ c^\top \theta \ \big| \ A_E \theta = b_E,\ A_I \theta \leq b_I \bigr\}.
\end{equation}

We focus on a setting where $A = (A_E', A_I')'$, $b$, and $c$ are potentially unknown and must be estimated from data. To estimate $v$, we consider the sample analogue of LP~\ref{eqnPW1}, given by
\begin{equation}
\label{eqnPW2}
\hat{v} = \min\bigl\{ \hat{c}^\top \theta \ \big| \ \hat{A}_E \theta = \hat{b}_E,\ \hat{A}_I \theta \leq \hat{b}_I \bigr\},
\end{equation}
where $(\hat{A}, \hat{b}, \hat{c})$ is a consistent estimator of $(A, b, c)$. In general, even if the population LP~\ref{eqnPW1} is feasible and has a finite value, the sample LP~\ref{eqnPW2} may be infeasible with probability bounded away from zero as the sample size $N$ tends to infinity. 

To address this issue, we impose Assumption~\ref{A1}, the Mangasarian-Fromovitz Constraint Qualification (MFCQ), which provides the minimal regularity condition required to ensure that the feasible region $\hat{\mathcal{P}}$ of LP~\ref{eqnPW2} is nonempty with probability approaching one as $N \to \infty$ (see Lemmas 2 and 3 in \citealt{SR2}).

Using a dual characterization of the MFCQ, we propose in Proposition~\ref{prop3} below a preprocessing step that transforms any LP that may not satisfy the MFCQ condition into an equivalent LP that does. As a result, Theorem~1 below, the main result of this section, applies broadly.

\begin{assumption}[Mangasarian-Fromovitz Constraint Qualification]
\label{A1}
The matrix $A_E$ has full row rank, and there exists a point $\theta_0$ such that $A_E \theta_0 = b_E$ and $A_I \theta_0 < b_I$.
\end{assumption}

We consider the following assumption, which guarantees that the values $v$ of our LP is finite. 
\begin{assumption}
\label{A1'}
 The feasible region 
\[
{\cal P}=\{\theta \in \mathbb{R}^d \ | \ A_E \theta=b_E \text{ and } A_I\theta \leq b_I \}
\]
is compact.
\end{assumption}

we show in the proof of Theorem \ref{thm1} that Assumptions \ref{A1} and \ref{A1'} imply that $\hat{v}$ is finite with probability approaching 1 as N tends to infinity. 

The dual of the LP \ref{eqnPW1} is given by 
\begin{equation}
\label{eqnPW3}
\max\left\{ \lambda_E^\top b_E + \lambda_I^\top b_I \ \middle| \ \lambda_I \leq 0, \ A_E^\top \lambda_E + A_I^\top \lambda_I = c \right\}.
\end{equation}
Let $\hat{{\cal P}}$ be defined by
\[
\hat{{\cal P}}:=\{\theta \in \mathbb{R}^d \ | \ \hat{A}_E\theta=\hat{b}_E,\ \hat{A}_I\theta\leq \hat{b}_I \}.
\]
By convention, we set $\hat{v}=+\infty$ if $\hat{{\cal P}}$ is empty.  When $\hat{{\cal P}}$ is nonempty, define $\hat{S}$
and $\hat{\Delta}$ as follows: 
\begin{equation}
\label{eqnS}
\hat{S}=\Bigl\{\theta \in \mathbb{R}^d \ | \ \hat{A}_E\theta=\hat{b}_E,\ \hat{A}_I\theta\leq \hat{b}_I, \ \hat{c}^T\theta \leq \hat{v}+\kappa_N/\sqrt{N} \Bigr\}
\end{equation}
and 
\begin{equation}
\label{eqnD}
\hat{\Delta}=\Bigl\{\lambda=(\lambda_E,\lambda_I) \in \mathbb{R}^p \ | \ \hat{A}_E^T\lambda_E+\hat{A}_I^T\lambda_I=\hat{c}, \ \lambda_I\leq 0, \lambda_E^T\hat{b}_E +\lambda_I ^T \hat{b}_I\geq \hat{v}-\kappa_N/\sqrt{N} \Bigr\},
\end{equation}
for some sequence of constants $\kappa_N$ such that $\kappa_N\to \infty$ and $\kappa_N/\sqrt{N}\to 0$. The sets $\hat{S}$ and $\hat{\Delta}$ can be viewed as estimators of the optimal solution sets of the primal and dual LPs, respectively.

Let \( d = \operatorname{vec}(A, b, c) \), and let \( \hat{d} \) denote the sample counterpart of \( d \). Let \( d_{\mathrm{Pr}} \) denote the Prokhorov metric for weak convergence. The following assumption requires that \( \sqrt{N}(\hat{d} - d) \) converges in distribution to a limiting law \( \mathbb{G} \), and that there exists a bootstrap-type estimator \( \zeta^*_N \) which consistently approximates this asymptotic distribution.

\begin{assumption}
\label{A2}
Suppose that $d_{Pr}\Bigl(\sqrt{N}(\hat{d} - d), \mathbb{G}\Bigr) \to 0$, and $d_{Pr}\Bigl({\cal L}\bigl(\zeta^*_N \mid \mathbb{W}\bigr), \mathbb{G}\Bigr) \xrightarrow{p} 0$, where $\zeta^*_N$ is a random variable with distribution that is completely determined from the data.
\end{assumption}

We now state the main result of this section, it builds on ideas from \cite{AS} and \cite{ZFAS} to constuct a pointwise valid inference procedure for the distribution of $\sqrt{N}(\hat{v}-v)$. The bootstrap procedure we propose is computationally straightforward, as it requires solving only a single LP in each bootstrap iteration. In fact, leveraging LP duality, the min-max optimization problem can be reformulated as a linear program. The proof is provided in Section \ref{pthm1} of the appendix.

\begin{theorem}
\label{thm1}
Suppose that Assumptions \ref{A1}, \ref{A1'} and \ref{A2} hold. Then 
\[
d_{\text{Pr}}\Biggl({\cal L}\left( \min_{\theta \in \hat{S}}\max_{\lambda \in \hat{\Delta}} \langle \zeta_c^*, \theta \rangle +\lambda^T\left(\zeta_b^*-\zeta_A^* \theta \right)| \mathbb{W}\right),\  \min_{\theta \in S_0} \max_{\lambda \in \Delta_0}  \langle \zeta_c, \theta\rangle +\lambda^T\left(\zeta_b-\zeta_A \theta \right)\Biggl)=o_p(1)
\]
where $(\zeta_c,\zeta_b,\zeta_A)\overset{d}{\sim} \mathbb{G}$.
\end{theorem}

When Assumption~\ref{A1} is not satisfied, but it is known that the ball \( \{ \lambda \mid \|\lambda\|_{\infty} \leq M \} \) has a nonempty intersection with the solution set of the dual LP~\ref{eqnPW3}, the following proposition constructs an LP that has the same value as the original LP~\ref{eqnPW1} and satisfies the MFCQ condition. This result follows from Lemma~\ref{lemMFCQ}, which establishes that the MFCQ condition holds if and only if the set of optimal solutions to the dual LP is bounded (see parts (a) and (b) of Theorem 1 in \citealt{SR2}). The proposition is derived by first adding the constraint \( \|\lambda\|_{\infty} \leq M \) to the feasible region of the dual LP \ref{eqnPW3}. The resulting modified dual LP has the same optimal value as the original LP. Taking the dual of this modified dual then yields a new primal LP that satisfies the MFCQ condition and retains the same optimal value. 

\begin{proposition}
\label{prop3}
Suppose that the LP~\ref{eqnPW1} is feasible and has a finite optimal value. If there exists a constant \( M > 0 \) such that
\[
\|\lambda\|_{\infty} \leq M \quad \text{for some } 
\lambda \in \argmin \left\{ \lambda_E^\top b_E + \lambda_I^\top b_I \ \middle| \ \lambda_I \leq 0, \ A_E^\top \lambda_E + A_I^\top \lambda_I = c \right\},
\]
then the optimal value \( v \) of LP~\ref{eqnPW1} also satisfies
\begin{equation}
\label{eqnPW4}
v = \min \left\{ \theta^\top c + M \mathbbm{1}^\top x + M \mathbbm{1}^\top (y + z) \ \middle| \ 
\begin{aligned}
& x \geq 0, \ y \geq 0, \ z \geq 0, \\
& A_E\theta + y - z = b_E, \\
& A_I\theta + x \geq b_I
\end{aligned}
\right\},
\end{equation}
and the above reformulated LP, which includes auxiliary variables \( x \), \( y \), and \( z \), satisfies the MFCQ condition.
\end{proposition}

\begin{remark}
A little bit of algebra shows that the reformulated LP can be written as 
\[
v=\min_{\theta} c^\top \theta +M (b_I-A_I\theta)_++M\|b_E-A_E\theta\|_1.
\]
When no inequality constraints are present, this yields the \textit{penalty function estimators} considered in \cite{AV}. The penalty function estimator of the value of the LP studied in \cite{AV} can therefore be interpreted as a method for transforming the original LP into one that satisfies the MFCQ condition. Moreover, the preceding proposition provides a general framework for constructing such \textit{penalty function estimators}. Specifically, for any polytope ${\cal C} = \{\lambda \mid H\lambda \leq h \}$ that contains the origin in its interior, we can construct a corresponding penalty function estimator as follows: first, add the constraint \( H\lambda \leq Mh \) to the feasible region of the dual LP~\ref{eqnPW3}, where \( M > 0 \) is a constant that serves as a tuning parameter. Note that if \( M \) is chosen large enough so that an optimal solution \( \lambda \) to the dual LP~\ref{eqnPW3} satisfies \( H\lambda \leq Mh \), then the value of the modified dual LP remains equal to the value of the original dual LP~\ref{eqnPW3}. Taking the dual of this modified dual LP yields a primal LP that satisfies the MFCQ condition and produces a penalty function estimator of the original LP value \( v \).
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}
In Proposition~\ref{prop3}, the bound \( M \) on a solution to the dual LP may not be known. \cite{AV} derives and consistently estimates the asymptotic distribution of \( \hat{v} \), obtained by solving the sample counterpart to LP~\ref{eqnPW4}, where \( M \) is allowed to grow with the sample size \( N \) at an appropriate rate.
\end{remark}



