\begin{proof}[\bf{Proof of Theorem \ref{thmSSFSST1}}]
\label{pthmSSFSST1}
The proof of equation~\ref{eqnSSFSST5} is identical to that of equation \ref{eqnSSARP6} in Theorem \ref{thmSSARP1}. For the proof of equation \ref{eqnSSFSST4}, we can proceed, with very minor changes, as in Steps~1 and ~2 of the proof of Section \ref{pthmSSARP1} (the proof of Theorem \ref{thmSSARP1}). However, Step~3 in Section \ref{pthmSSARP1} is different in the present context, in view of the new complications that arise from the sets ${\cal D}(Q)$ potentially not having extreme points. As the proof of the analogue of Step 3 in the present context is more similar to the one in Section \ref{pthmSSARP1} under assumption \ref{assSSFSST1}, we only establish Step~3 under Assumption \ref{assSSFSST2}, which is more closely related to the assumptions in \cite{FSST}.  Also, since $ {\cal D}(Q)$ is no longer restricted to be a subset of the positive orthant, the constraint $\|D\lambda\|_1 \leq 1$ is now equivalent to a system of inequalities $H D\lambda \leq \mathbbm{1}$, for some matrix $H$ and conformable vector $\mathbbm{1}$, as in Lemma~\ref{lemEPC}.

This yields a Lagrangian for the unperturbed LP (with a similar form for the perturbed case):
\[
L(\lambda, x, y) = b^\top \lambda + x^\top (\mathbbm{1} - H D \lambda) - y^\top A^\top \lambda,
\]
where $x, y \geq 0$ are Lagrange multipliers. With these modifications, we redefine the events $E_N(Q)$ as follows:\footnote{This redefinition effectively appends the event $\bigl\{\hat{b} - b(Q) \in \operatorname{Range}(\Sigma(Q))\bigr\}$ to the previously defined event $E_N(Q)$. Assumption~\ref{assSSFSST2} ensures that we continue to have $\underline{\lim}_{N \to \infty} \inf_{Q \in \mathcal{Q}} Q(E_N(Q)) = 1$.}

\[
E_N(Q) := \left\{ \mathbb{W} \;\middle|\; 
\begin{array}{l}
\|\hat{D} - D\| \leq \alpha_N,\quad
d_{\mathrm{Pr}}\bigl(\mathcal{L}(\zeta^* \mid \mathbb{W}), \mathcal{N}(0, \Sigma(Q))\bigr) \leq \alpha_N,\\[0.5em]
\sqrt{N} \|\hat{b} - b(Q)\| \leq M_N,\quad
\hat{b} - b(Q) \in \operatorname{Range}(\Sigma(Q))
\end{array}
\right\}.
\]

 Steps~1 and~2 of Theorem~\ref{thmSSARP1} go through. We now turn to Step~3, which verifies the anti-concentration condition (equation \ref{eqnPSSARP8} in the proof of Theorem \ref{thmSSARP1}) needed in Step~2, in the setting of the present theorem. Throughout the argument below, we use $C$ to represent a generic constant that does not depend on $Q \in \mathcal{Q}$ or on $N$, for all sufficiently large $N$. The value of $C$ may vary from line to line.

\underline{Step 3} We now establish equation \ref{eqnPSSARP8} under Assumptions \ref{assSSAS1}, \ref{assSSAS3},\ref{assSSARP1}, \ref{assSSFSST0}, and \ref{assSSFSST2}. Suppose that the event $\{\hat{T}_N>\hat{c}_N(1-\alpha)\}\cap E_N(Q)$ occurs, where $E_N(Q)$ is as defined above. Then, we necessarily have $\hat{v}>0$, with $\hat{v}$ finite.\footnote{We recall here that from the discussion in the paragraph that follows equation \ref{eqnSSFSST3}, if $\hat{v}=\infty$, then $Q(\hat{v}=+\infty)=1$ and we always reject the null. Thus it suffices to only consider DGPs such that $\hat{v}$ is always finite.}

 We now show that it must be the case that the optimal value is achieved at an extreme point of $\hat{{\cal D}}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp$. Indeed, by the Weyl-Minkowski decomposition theorem, the polyhedron $\hat{{\cal D}}(Q)$ can be decomposed as the Minkowski sum of a pointed polyhedron (i.e., a polyhedron with at least one extreme point, or equivalently a polyhedron that does not contain a line), and the lineality space of $\hat{D}(Q)$ (defined as the set of all $z\in \mathbb{R}^p$ such that for all $\lambda\in \hat{{\cal D}}$, $\lambda+t z\in \hat{{\cal D}}$ for all $t\in \mathbb{R}$). In the present context, the decomposition can be explicitly given by
\[
\hat{{\cal D}}(Q)=[\hat{{\cal D}}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp]+{\cal L}(Q),
\]
where $+$ denotes the Minkowski sum, and ${\cal L}(Q)$ is the lineality space of ${\cal D}(Q)$ (and $\hat{{\cal D}}(Q)$) \footnote{Note that since $D$ and $\hat{D}$ have the same nonzero diagonal elements, the conditions $\|D\lambda\|_1=0$, $\|\hat{D}\lambda\|_1=0$, and $\Pi^u(\lambda)=0$ are equivalent.}given by the subspace
\[
{\cal L}(Q)=\bigl\{\lambda \in \mathbb{R}^p\mid A^\top \lambda=0, \ \Pi^u(\lambda)=0\bigr\}.
\]
Indeed, for  $\lambda \in \mathbb{R}^p$, if we let $\Pi_{\cal L}(\lambda)$ denote the projection of $\lambda$ on the subspace ${\cal L}(Q)$, then $\lambda-\Pi_{\cal L}(\lambda)\in \hat{\cal D}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp$ for all $\lambda\in \hat{{\cal D}}(Q)$, yielding the desired decomposition. We now show that since $\hat{v}$ is finite, we must have
\[
\hat{v}=\max\{\hat{b}^\top \lambda\mid \lambda \in \hat{{\cal D}}(Q)\}=\max\{\hat{b}^\top \lambda\mid \lambda \in \hat{{\cal D}}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp\}.
\]
This follows since $\hat{v}$ being finite implies that we must have $\hat{b}^\top z=0$ for all $z\in {\cal L}(Q)$, since we must have $\hat{b}^\top (\lambda+tz)\leq \hat{v}$ for all $\lambda \in \hat{{\cal D}}(Q)$ and $t\in \mathbb{R}$. Hence, the set of optimal values contains the set 
\[
\arg\max\{\hat{b}^\top \lambda\mid \lambda \in \hat{{\cal D}}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp\},
\]
which is a face of $\hat{{\cal D}}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp$. As $\hat{{\cal D}}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp$ is a pointed polyhedron,\footnote{The set $\hat{{\cal D}}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp$ is a pointed polyhedron, since any vector in its lineality is necessarily in the lineality of $\hat{{\cal D}}$, and thus in ${\cal L}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp=\{0\}$} and as such, any face of $\hat{{\cal D}}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp$ contains an extreme point, which cannot be zero, since $\hat{v}>0$. In conclusion, on the event $\{\hat{T}_N>\hat{c}_N(1-\alpha)\}\cap E_N(Q)$, there must exist $\hat{\lambda} \in \operatorname{extr}(\hat{{\cal D}}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp)\setminus \{0\}$ such that $\hat{b}^\top \hat{\lambda}=\hat{v}$. We will work with such a $\hat{\lambda}$ below.

Now since ${\cal L}(Q)^\perp$ is a subspace, it admits a polyhedral representation of the form
\[
{\cal L}(Q)^\perp=\{\lambda\in \mathbb{R}^p\mid M\lambda=0\},
\]
for some matrix $M$, leading to a representation of $\hat{{\cal D}}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp$ given by
\[
\hat{{\cal D}}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp=\{\lambda \in \mathbb{R}^p\mid A^\top \lambda\leq 0,\ M\lambda=0,\ \|\hat{D}\lambda\|_1\leq 1\}.
\]
The latter representation comforms with the representation of the polytopes discussed in Lemma \ref{lemEPC}, and equation \ref{eqnlemEPC2} implies that for all sufficiently large $N$, for all $Q\in {\cal Q}_0$, and for all $\lambda \in \operatorname{extr}(\hat{{\cal D}}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp)\setminus \{0\}$, there exists $\lambda'\in \operatorname{extr}({\cal D}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp)\setminus \{0\}$ such that 
\begin{equation}
\label{eqnPSSFSST1}
\lambda-\lambda'=(1-\frac{1}{\|D\lambda\|_1})\lambda \quad \quad \text{and}\quad \quad \|\Pi^u(\lambda)-\Pi^u(\lambda')\|\leq C\alpha_N,
\end{equation}
where we have used the fact that $\|\hat{D}-D\|\leq \alpha_N$ on $E_N(Q)$, and that $\sup_{\lambda \in \hat{{\cal D}}}\|\Pi^u(\lambda)\|\leq C$, as in equation \ref{eqnPSSARP4}. Thus our in sample optimal solution $\hat{\lambda}$ is the positive scalar multiple of an extreme point $\tilde{\lambda}$ of ${\cal D}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp\setminus \{0\}$, and 
\begin{equation}
\label{eqnPSSFSST2}
\|\Pi^u(\hat{\lambda})-\Pi^u(\tilde{\lambda})\|\leq C\alpha_N.
\end{equation}

We claim that Assumption \ref{assSSFSST2} implies that on the event $E_N(Q)$, we must have $\tilde{\lambda}^\top \Sigma(Q)\tilde{\lambda}\geq \rho$, with $\rho$ as in Assumption \ref{assSSFSST2}. Indeed, by the definition of the event $E_N(Q)$, we have $\hat{b}-b\in \operatorname{Range}(\Sigma(Q))$ on $E_N(Q)$. But then $\tilde{\lambda}$ cannot be an extreme point of ${\cal D}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp\setminus \{0\}$ such that 
\begin{equation}
\label{eqnPSSFSST3}
\tilde{\lambda}^\top \Sigma(Q)\tilde{\lambda}=0
\end{equation}
as the latter would imply that $\tilde{\lambda}\in \operatorname{Range}(\Sigma(Q))^\perp$ and that $\tilde{\lambda}^\top \hat{b}=\tilde{\lambda}^\top b\leq 0$, since $b^\top \lambda\leq 0$ for all $\lambda \in {\cal D}(Q)$ (and thus $\hat{{\cal D}}(Q)$, by homogeneity). But the latter would contradict $\hat{b}^\top \tilde{\lambda}>0$, as $\hat{v}=\hat{\lambda}^\top \hat{b}>0$, and $\tilde{\lambda}$ is a positive scalar multiple of $\hat{\lambda}$. We thus conclude that for all large N, for $Q\in {\cal Q}_0$, and on the event $\{\hat{T}_N>\hat{c}_N(1-\alpha)\}\cap E_N(Q)$, there must exist a $\hat{\lambda}\in \operatorname{extr}(\hat{{\cal D}}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp)\setminus \{0\} $
and $\tilde{\lambda}\in  \operatorname{extr}({\cal D}(Q)\cap {\cal L}(Q)^\perp)\setminus \{0\}$, such that equations \ref{eqnPSSFSST2} and \ref{eqnPSSFSST3} hold. As in equation \ref{eqnPSSARP013}, we then have
\begin{equation*}
\label{eqnPSSFSST4}
\begin{aligned}
\hat{\lambda}^\top \Sigma(Q)\hat{\lambda}&=\Pi^u(\hat{\lambda})^\top \Sigma(Q)\Pi^u(\hat{\lambda})=\|\Sigma(Q)^{1/2}\Pi^u(\hat{\lambda})\|^2\\
&\geq (1/2)\|\Sigma(Q)^{1/2}\Pi^u(\tilde{\lambda})\|^2-\|\Sigma(Q)^{1/2}(\Pi^u(\hat{\lambda})-\Pi^u(\tilde{\lambda}))\|^2\\
&=(1/2)\tilde{\lambda}^\top \Sigma(Q)\tilde{\lambda}-\|\Sigma(Q)^{1/2}(\Pi^u(\hat{\lambda})-\Pi^u(\tilde{\lambda}))\|^2\\
&\geq (1/2)\tilde{\lambda}^\top \Sigma(Q)\tilde{\lambda}-C\alpha_N^2.
\end{aligned}
\end{equation*}
Since $\hat{\lambda}$ is in $\hat{\Delta}$, the latter inequality implies that for all sufficiently large $N$, for $Q\in {\cal Q}_0$, and on the event $\{\hat{T}_N>\hat{c}_N(1-\alpha)\}\cap E_N(Q)$, the set $\hat{\Delta}$ contains an element $\hat{\lambda}$ that satisfies
\begin{equation}
\label{eqnPSSFSST4} 
\hat{\lambda}^\top \Sigma(Q)\hat{\lambda}\geq \rho/4.
\end{equation}
The anti-concentration condition \ref{eqnPSSARP8} (in the present context) then follows from Proposition \ref{prop1}, using the fact that the sets $\Pi^u(\hat{\Delta})$ are eventually uniformly bounded on the events $E_N(Q)$, and
\[
\sup_{\lambda \in \hat{\Delta}} \langle \zeta, \lambda \rangle=\sup_{\lambda \in \hat{\Delta}} \langle \Pi^u(\zeta), \Pi^u(\lambda) \rangle,
\]
where $\zeta\sim \mathcal{N}(0,\Sigma(Q))$.


\end{proof}
