\subsection{Grossman-Nir bound}
\label{sec:GN_bound}
After establishing that in some theories the three-body decay can be the dominant channel for long-lived neutral kaons, it would be useful to compare the three-body rate to the charged kaon decay rate.
Since the latter does not require any CP violation, the charged kaon decay usually provides a stronger probe of FV and can be used to place an upper bound on the neutral decay rates using the Grossmann-Nir (GN) bound~\cite{Grossman:1997sk}.
We are interested in the ratios,
\begin{align}
   \frac{\Gamma(K_L \to \pi^0 \pi^0 a)}{\Gamma(K^+ \to \pi^+  a)}\,, \;\;  \frac{\Gamma(K_L \to \pi^+ \pi^- a)}{\Gamma(K^+ \to \pi^+  a)},
\end{align}
which we can calculate by multiplying $R_0,R_\pm$ defined in \Eq{eq:ratio_defs} by a common proportionality factor,
\begin{align}  
\label{eq:GN_bound}
R_{\text{\tiny GN}} \equiv \frac{\Gamma(K_L \to \pi^0 a)}{\Gamma(K^+ \to \pi^+ a)}\,.
\end{align}
We can explicitly calculate $R_{\text{GN}}$ using the results of \Sec{sec:two_body} and assuming as before that $N_8$ is real,
\begin{align}  
R_{\text{\tiny GN}}  =& \frac{|\mathcal{M}(K_L \to \pi^0 a)|^2}{|\mathcal{M}(K^+ \to \pi^+ a)|^2} 
= \sin^2\theta+2\text{Im}\,\varepsilon \sin \theta (x+\cos\theta) +|\varepsilon|^2(x+\cos\theta)^2\,,
\end{align}
where we denoted
\begin{align}
    \kappa_{V}+N_8 (\mathcal{C}_1+\Delta \mathcal{C}_1) \equiv re^{i \theta}\;\;\;\; \text{and}\;\;\;\;x \equiv \frac{N_8 \Delta \mathcal{C}_1}{r}\,.
\end{align}
The ratio $R_{\text{GN}}$ is bounded by unity when neglecting kaon mixing ($\varepsilon\to0$), as it is assumed in the original derivation.
Interestingly, once mixing is taken into account and as $m_a \to m_\pi$, one finds that $x \gg1$ (see expressions for $\mathcal{C}_1$ and $\Delta\mathcal{C}_1$ in \App{app:Ci}).
The bound can then be significantly weakened once $ x \gtrsim |\varepsilon|^{-1}$.
This enhancement occurs as a result of resonant weak-induced mixing between the ALP and $\pi_0$. This causes the neutral kaon decay rate to spike (see the expression for $\Delta\mathcal C_1$ in Eq. (\ref{eq:DC1})), while the charged decay rate goes to a constant.
One should keep in mind, however, that depending on the value of the decay constant $f$, the small-angle approximation assumed for the calculation may break down if the ALP mass is sufficiently degenerate with the pion mass.

Assuming the maximal flavor violation scenario of \Sec{sec:max_FV}, $\kappa_V \gg N_8$, we identify $\theta \approx \theta_\kappa$.
An enhancement can occur if the ALP mass is tuned such that
$|m_a^2/m_\pi^2-1| \ll \varepsilon N_8/\kappa_V$.
Otherwise, we have $\varepsilon x \ll 1$, in which case $R_{\text{GN}}\cdot R_{0,\pm} \sim I_{0,\pm}(m_a)\sim10^{-3}$ if the coupling has a vanishing or an $\mathcal{O}(1)$ phase, since both decays do not require CP violation.
If the coupling is a pure phase $\theta_\kappa \sim \pi/2$, $R_{\text{\tiny GN}}$ is suppressed by $|\varepsilon|^2$.

In \Fig{fig:GN} we plot the ratio $\Gamma(K_L \to \pi^0 \pi^0 a)/\Gamma(K^+ \to \pi^+  a)$ for the different MFV scenarios as a function of ALP mass for fixed $f=10^3\,$GeV.
The small-angle approximation for this value of $f$ is reliable for $|m_a^2/m_\pi^2-1| \gg f_\pi/f \sim 10^{-4}$.
We find a visible enhancement in models in which $\tilde{c}_{WW}\neq 0$ or $\tilde{c}_{BB}\neq 0$.
The three-body decay in these models are dominated by the indirect weak contribution $N_8 \gg \kappa_V$, and the ratio can be order unity once the ALP mass is tuned with the pion mass at the $\mathcal{O}(1\%)$ level (see the insert in the figure). 
The remaining two models do not exhibit the same enhancement unless the ALP mass is finely tuned to the pion mass, e.g. $\mathcal{O}(10^{-10})$ for the $c_{tt}\neq 0$ model - well beyond the validity of the small-angle approximation.

